(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("k8s-exam",{"k8s-exam/the-exam-environment-speed-setup-and-the-docs":{"html":"\u003cp>The CKA and CKS are performance-based: you work in a remote terminal on real clusters, solve tasks, and are scored on the end state. The earlier lesson on exam strategy in the Kubernetes module covers what each exam tests. This lesson is about the working environment itself, because many candidates lose minutes to habits that a short setup would remove.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What the exam environment is like and how scoring works\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A two-minute setup of aliases, completion and editor settings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to switch contexts and never work on the wrong cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to search the documentation quickly\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-exam-works\">How the exam works\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Check the current candidate handbook on the Linux Foundation site before you book, because duration, passing score, Kubernetes version and allowed resources change over time. At the time of writing, each exam is about two hours, made up of separate tasks with different weights, and you must reach a passing score of around two thirds. Tasks run on several clusters, and each task tells you which context to use. You may open one extra browser tab for the official documentation, and for the CKS the documentation of a few named tools. You may not use outside notes or your own search engine.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Because scoring checks the end state, partial work counts on many tasks. That is why order matters: take the quick, high-value tasks first and flag the slow ones.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-domains-and-their-weights\">The domains and their weights\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The curriculum is published by the CNCF and weights change from time to time. Use the current numbers from the official curriculum; the ones below are typical and give the shape.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>CKA domain\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Typical weight\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Troubleshooting\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>30 percent\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cluster architecture, installation and configuration\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>25 percent\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Services and networking\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>20 percent\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Workloads and scheduling\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>15 percent\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Storage\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>10 percent\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>CKS domain\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Typical weight\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Minimize microservice vulnerabilities\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>20 percent\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Supply chain security\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>20 percent\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Monitoring, logging and runtime security\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>20 percent\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cluster hardening\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>15 percent\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>System hardening\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>15 percent\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cluster setup\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>10 percent\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The practical lesson of the weights: troubleshooting alone is nearly a third of the CKA, so spend a corresponding share of your practice time on it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-two-minute-setup\">The two-minute setup\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The terminal usually has \u003ccode>kubectl\u003c/code> completion and an alias already available, but do not assume. Check, and add what is missing. A typical setup:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"alias k=kubectl\nsource &lt;(kubectl completion bash)\ncomplete -o default -F __start_kubectl k\nexport do=&quot;--dry-run=client -o yaml&quot;\nexport now=&quot;--force --grace-period=0&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">alias\u003c/span> k=kubectl\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">source\u003c/span> &lt;(kubectl completion bash)\ncomplete -o default -F __start_kubectl k\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">export\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--dry-run=client -o yaml&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">export\u003c/span> now=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--force --grace-period=0&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Then set up the editor. For vim, a \u003ccode>~/.vimrc\u003c/code> with these lines saves a lot of YAML pain:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cat &gt; ~/.vimrc &lt;&lt;'VIMRC'\nset expandtab\nset tabstop=2\nset shiftwidth=2\nset number\nVIMRC\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> &gt; ~/.vimrc &lt;&lt;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;VIMRC&#x27;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> expandtab\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> tabstop=2\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> shiftwidth=2\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> number\nVIMRC\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Spaces instead of tabs matters because YAML rejects tab indentation. Practise until typing \u003ccode>k run web --image=nginx $do &gt; pod.yaml\u003c/code> is automatic.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"contexts-the-most-expensive-mistake\">Contexts: the most expensive mistake\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every task starts with a command that selects the cluster, such as \u003ccode>kubectl config use-context k8s-c2\u003c/code>. Run it first, every time. Doing a correct task on the wrong cluster scores zero and may break another task. Check with \u003ccode>kubectl config current-context\u003c/code> before long edits. Notice also that some tasks tell you to connect to a node with \u003ccode>ssh\u003c/code>; work there only as long as the task needs, then exit back to the base terminal before starting the next task.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"finding-answers-in-the-docs\">Finding answers in the docs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The allowed documentation is the Kubernetes site, and its search works well if you give it the resource name. Habits that help:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Search for the task keyword plus the object, for example &quot;persistentvolume&quot; or &quot;network policy&quot;\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use the &quot;Tasks&quot; pages, which are written as copy-paste examples\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Copy YAML from the docs and edit it, rather than typing from memory\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use \u003ccode>kubectl explain pod.spec.containers --recursive\u003c/code> offline in the terminal for field names; it is faster than searching\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"time-budget\">Time budget\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set a rule such as: no task takes more than its share of the clock. If a task is worth 4 percent and you have spent six minutes, flag it and move on. Return at the end. Keep a short list of flagged tasks in a notes box if the interface provides one. Leave time at the end to re-read the question text of each flagged task, because a missed requirement such as a specific file path is easy to fix when you reread.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"tasks = [\n    (&quot;create pod with label&quot;, 2, 3),\n    (&quot;etcd backup&quot;, 7, 8),\n    (&quot;fix kubelet&quot;, 8, 12),\n    (&quot;network policy&quot;, 5, 7),\n]\nminutes_left = 120\nfor name, weight, est in sorted(tasks, key=lambda t: t[2] / t[1]):\n    print(f&quot;{name:24} weight {weight}  est {est} min  minutes per point {est / weight:.1f}&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">tasks = [\n    (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;create pod with label&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>),\n    (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;etcd backup&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">7\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8\u003c/span>),\n    (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;fix kubelet&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">12\u003c/span>),\n    (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;network policy&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">7\u003c/span>),\n]\nminutes_left = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">120\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> name, weight, est \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sorted\u003c/span>(tasks, key=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">lambda\u003c/span> t: t[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>] / t[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>]):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{name:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">24\u003c/span>}\u003c/span> weight \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{weight}\u003c/span>  est \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{est}\u003c/span> min  minutes per point \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{est / weight:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.1\u003c/span>f}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Order by minutes per point and you will finish easy points first. This is a planning aid for practice, not something to run in the exam.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"ctx&gt;read;read&gt;gen;gen&gt;apply;apply&gt;flag\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">First minutes of an exam task\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ctx\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Select context\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"read\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Read the whole task\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"gen\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Generate or copy YAML\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"apply\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Apply and verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"flag\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Flag if slow\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Select context to Read the whole task; 2, Read the whole task to Generate or copy YAML; 3, Generate or copy YAML to Apply and verify; 4, Apply and verify to Flag if slow.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ctx\">\u003cdt>Select context \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Run the use-context command the task gives.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Working on the wrong cluster.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"read\">\u003cdt>Read the whole task \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Note names, paths and namespaces.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing a path or flag in the text.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"gen\">\u003cdt>Generate or copy YAML \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Imperative commands and docs examples.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Typing long YAML from memory.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"apply\">\u003cdt>Apply and verify \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Check with get and describe.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not verifying the end state.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"flag\">\u003cdt>Flag if slow \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Move on, come back later.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Sinking time into one task.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-right-answer-on-the-wrong-cluster\">Incident: The right answer on the wrong cluster\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A candidate spent twelve minutes fixing a broken node, verified that the fix worked, and scored nothing for the task.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl config current-context\nkubectl get nodes\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl config current-context\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">k8s-c1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME       STATUS   ROLES           AGE   VERSION\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">c1-cp1     Ready    control-plane   40d   v1.31.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The task had said to use context \u003ccode>k8s-c3\u003c/code>. The candidate had skipped the \u003ccode>use-context\u003c/code> line and worked on the cluster from the previous task. The fix was correct but applied to the wrong cluster, and the broken node on the right cluster stayed broken. The habit that prevents this is to paste the context command from the task text before doing anything else.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Skipping the context command at the start of each task\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Typing long YAML from scratch instead of generating or copying it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using tabs in YAML\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Spending too long on one low-value task\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting to leave a node or ssh session after a task\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"how-the-exam-works","text":"How the exam works"},{"id":"the-domains-and-their-weights","text":"The domains and their weights"},{"id":"the-two-minute-setup","text":"The two-minute setup"},{"id":"contexts-the-most-expensive-mistake","text":"Contexts: the most expensive mistake"},{"id":"finding-answers-in-the-docs","text":"Finding answers in the docs"},{"id":"time-budget","text":"Time budget"},{"id":"incident-the-right-answer-on-the-wrong-cluster","text":"Incident: The right answer on the wrong cluster"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"CKA certification","u":"https://training.linuxfoundation.org/certification/certified-kubernetes-administrator-cka/"},{"t":"CKS certification","u":"https://training.linuxfoundation.org/certification/certified-kubernetes-security-specialist/"},{"t":"kubectl cheat sheet","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/quick-reference/"}],"text":"The CKA and CKS are performance-based: you work in a remote terminal on real clusters, solve tasks, and are scored on the end state. The earlier lesson on exam strategy in the Kubernetes module covers what each exam tests. This lesson is about the working environment itself, because many candidates lose minutes to habits that a short setup would remove. What you will learn What the exam environment is like and how scoring works A two-minute setup of aliases, completion and editor settings How to switch contexts and never work on the wrong cluster How to search the documentation quickly How the exam works Check the current candidate handbook on the Linux Foundation site before you book, because duration, passing score, Kubernetes version and allowed resources change over time. At the time of writing, each exam is about two hours, made up of separate tasks with different weights, and you must reach a passing score of around two thirds. Tasks run on several clusters, and each task tells you which context to use. You may open one extra browser tab for the official documentation, and for the CKS the documentation of a few named tools. You may not use outside notes or your own search engine. Because scoring checks the end state, partial work counts on many tasks. That is why order matters: take the quick, high-value tasks first and flag the slow ones. The domains and their weights The curriculum is published by the CNCF and weights change from time to time. Use the current numbers from the official curriculum; the ones below are typical and give the shape. CKA domain Typical weight Troubleshooting 30 percent Cluster architecture, installation and configuration 25 percent Services and networking 20 percent Workloads and scheduling 15 percent Storage 10 percent CKS domain Typical weight Minimize microservice vulnerabilities 20 percent Supply chain security 20 percent Monitoring, logging and runtime security 20 percent Cluster hardening 15 percent System hardening 15 percent Cluster setup 10 percent The practical lesson of the weights: troubleshooting alone is nearly a third of the CKA, so spend a corresponding share of your practice time on it. The two-minute setup The terminal usually has kubectl completion and an alias already available, but do not assume. Check, and add what is missing. A typical setup: alias k=kubectl source \u003c(kubectl completion bash) complete -o default -F __start_kubectl k export do = \"--dry-run=client -o yaml\" export now= \"--force --grace-period=0\" Then set up the editor. For vim, a ~/.vimrc with these lines saves a lot of YAML pain: cat > ~/.vimrc \u003c\u003c &#x27;VIMRC&#x27; set expandtab set tabstop=2 set shiftwidth=2 set number VIMRC Spaces instead of tabs matters because YAML rejects tab indentation. Practise until typing k run web --image=nginx $do > pod.yaml is automatic. Contexts: the most expensive mistake Every task starts with a command that selects the cluster, such as kubectl config use-context k8s-c2 . Run it first, every time. Doing a correct task on the wrong cluster scores zero and may break another task. Check with kubectl config current-context before long edits. Notice also that some tasks tell you to connect to a node with ssh ; work there only as long as the task needs, then exit back to the base terminal before starting the next task. Finding answers in the docs The allowed documentation is the Kubernetes site, and its search works well if you give it the resource name. Habits that help: Search for the task keyword plus the object, for example \"persistentvolume\" or \"network policy\" Use the \"Tasks\" pages, which are written as copy-paste examples Copy YAML from the docs and edit it, rather than typing from memory Use kubectl explain pod.spec.containers --recursive offline in the terminal for field names; it is faster than searching Time budget Set a rule such as: no task takes more than its share of the clock. If a task is worth 4 percent and you have spent six minutes, flag it and move on. Return "},"k8s-exam/imperative-kubectl-and-fast-yaml":{"html":"\u003cp>Declarative YAML is the right way to manage clusters over time. In an exam, and in many incident situations, speed matters more. kubectl can create most common objects imperatively and print the YAML for you to adjust. Learning this set of commands is the best time investment for a hands-on test.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Imperative commands for the common objects\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The dry-run pattern for generating YAML\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Quick edits, patches and set commands\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Output formats that answer questions fast\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"creating-objects-imperatively\">Creating objects imperatively\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl run web --image=nginx:1.27 --port=80 --labels=app=web\nkubectl create deployment api --image=nginx:1.27 --replicas=3\nkubectl expose deployment api --port=80 --target-port=8080 --type=ClusterIP\nkubectl create configmap app-config --from-literal=mode=prod --from-file=settings.ini\nkubectl create secret generic db-pass --from-literal=password=changeme\nkubectl create job once --image=busybox:1.36 -- echo hello\nkubectl create cronjob tick --image=busybox:1.36 --schedule=&quot;*/5 * * * *&quot; -- date\nkubectl create namespace dev\nkubectl create serviceaccount deployer -n dev\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl run web --image=nginx:1.27 --port=80 --labels=app=web\nkubectl create deployment api --image=nginx:1.27 --replicas=3\nkubectl expose deployment api --port=80 --target-port=8080 --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>=ClusterIP\nkubectl create configmap app-config --from-literal=mode=prod --from-file=settings.ini\nkubectl create secret generic db-pass --from-literal=password=changeme\nkubectl create job once --image=busybox:1.36 -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> hello\nkubectl create cronjob tick --image=busybox:1.36 --schedule=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;*/5 * * * *&quot;\u003c/span> -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">date\u003c/span>\nkubectl create namespace dev\nkubectl create serviceaccount deployer -n dev\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Anything after \u003ccode>--\u003c/code> is the container command. Pin image tags, as in real life.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-dry-run-pattern\">The dry-run pattern\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When you need a field that no flag provides, generate YAML and edit it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl create deployment api --image=nginx:1.27 --replicas=3 --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; api.yaml\nvim api.yaml\nkubectl apply -f api.yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl create deployment api --image=nginx:1.27 --replicas=3 --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; api.yaml\nvim api.yaml\nkubectl apply -f api.yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--dry-run=client\u003c/code> prints the object without sending it to the server. With \u003ccode>-o yaml\u003c/code> you get a valid starting file. Add what you need, such as a volume or a security context, and apply. Use \u003ccode>--dry-run=server\u003c/code> when you want the API server&#39;s validation without creating anything.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"changing-what-exists\">Changing what exists\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl set image deployment/api nginx=nginx:1.27.1\nkubectl set resources deployment/api --limits=cpu=500m,memory=256Mi --requests=cpu=100m,memory=128Mi\nkubectl scale deployment/api --replicas=5\nkubectl label pod web tier=frontend\nkubectl annotate pod web owner=team-a\nkubectl rollout status deployment/api\nkubectl rollout undo deployment/api\nkubectl edit deployment api\nkubectl patch deployment api -p '{&quot;spec&quot;:{&quot;replicas&quot;:2}}'\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> image deployment/api nginx=nginx:1.27.1\nkubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> resources deployment/api --limits=cpu=500m,memory=256Mi --requests=cpu=100m,memory=128Mi\nkubectl scale deployment/api --replicas=5\nkubectl label pod web tier=frontend\nkubectl annotate pod web owner=team-a\nkubectl rollout status deployment/api\nkubectl rollout undo deployment/api\nkubectl edit deployment api\nkubectl patch deployment api -p \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;spec&quot;:{&quot;replicas&quot;:2}}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>kubectl edit\u003c/code> opens the live object in your editor; saving applies it. For fields that are immutable, such as most pod spec fields, you must delete and recreate, or use \u003ccode>kubectl replace --force -f file.yaml\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-output-quickly\">Reading output quickly\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -o wide\nkubectl get pods --show-labels\nkubectl get pods -l app=web\nkubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase!=Running\nkubectl get pods --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp\nkubectl get pod web -o jsonpath='{.status.podIP}'\nkubectl get nodes -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,CPU:.status.capacity.cpu\nkubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\nkubectl describe pod web\nkubectl logs web --previous\nkubectl explain pod.spec.containers.securityContext\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get pods -o wide\nkubectl get pods --show-labels\nkubectl get pods -l app=web\nkubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase!=Running\nkubectl get pods --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp\nkubectl get pod web -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.status.podIP}&#x27;\u003c/span>\nkubectl get nodes -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,CPU:.status.capacity.cpu\nkubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\nkubectl describe pod web\nkubectl logs web --previous\nkubectl explain pod.spec.containers.securityContext\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>JSONPath and custom columns answer tasks such as &quot;write the names of all nodes to a file&quot; without manual copying. Redirect to a file with \u003ccode>&gt;\u003c/code> and check it with \u003ccode>cat\u003c/code> afterwards.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"working-inside-pods\">Working inside pods\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl exec -it web -- sh\nkubectl run tmp --rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- wget -qO- http://api\nkubectl cp web:/etc/nginx/nginx.conf ./nginx.conf\nkubectl port-forward svc/api 8080:80\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> -it web -- sh\nkubectl run tmp --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- wget -qO- http://api\nkubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cp\u003c/span> web:/etc/nginx/nginx.conf ./nginx.conf\nkubectl port-forward svc/api 8080:80\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The temporary pod pattern with \u003ccode>--rm -it --restart=Never\u003c/code> is the quickest way to test service names and network policies from inside the cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"know-your-limits\">Know your limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Imperative commands cover the common cases. Pod volumes, init containers, probes, affinity, tolerations and most security settings need YAML. Know the dry-run pattern well enough that adding a block to generated YAML takes seconds. Keep a mental library of the three or four YAML snippets you use most, and copy the rest from the docs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-quick-sanity-check\">A quick sanity check\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Before applying a generated file, read it once. Check the namespace, the name, labels matching selectors, and that no leftover fields such as \u003ccode>status: {}\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>creationTimestamp: null\u003c/code> cause problems. Those two are harmless, but a wrong namespace is not.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import json\n\n# check that a generated deployment's selector matches its pod template labels\nd = json.loads(&quot;&quot;&quot;{\n &quot;spec&quot;: {&quot;selector&quot;: {&quot;matchLabels&quot;: {&quot;app&quot;: &quot;api&quot;}},\n          &quot;template&quot;: {&quot;metadata&quot;: {&quot;labels&quot;: {&quot;app&quot;: &quot;api&quot;, &quot;tier&quot;: &quot;back&quot;}}}}\n}&quot;&quot;&quot;)\nsel = d[&quot;spec&quot;][&quot;selector&quot;][&quot;matchLabels&quot;]\nlabels = d[&quot;spec&quot;][&quot;template&quot;][&quot;metadata&quot;][&quot;labels&quot;]\nprint(&quot;selector matches template:&quot;, all(labels.get(k) == v for k, v in sel.items()))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> json\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># check that a generated deployment&#x27;s selector matches its pod template labels\u003c/span>\nd = json.loads(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;&quot;{\n &quot;spec&quot;: {&quot;selector&quot;: {&quot;matchLabels&quot;: {&quot;app&quot;: &quot;api&quot;}},\n          &quot;template&quot;: {&quot;metadata&quot;: {&quot;labels&quot;: {&quot;app&quot;: &quot;api&quot;, &quot;tier&quot;: &quot;back&quot;}}}}\n}&quot;&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>)\nsel = d[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;spec&quot;\u003c/span>][\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;selector&quot;\u003c/span>][\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;matchLabels&quot;\u003c/span>]\nlabels = d[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;spec&quot;\u003c/span>][\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;template&quot;\u003c/span>][\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;metadata&quot;\u003c/span>][\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;labels&quot;\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;selector matches template:&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">all\u003c/span>(labels.get(k) == v \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> k, v \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> sel.items()))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"cmd&gt;dry;dry&gt;edit;edit&gt;api;api&gt;chk\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">From command to resource\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cmd\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">kubectl create\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dry\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Dry run output\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"edit\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Edit the file\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">API server\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"chk\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, kubectl create to Dry run output; 2, Dry run output to Edit the file; 3, Edit the file to API server; 4, API server to Verify.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cmd\">\u003cdt>kubectl create \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Imperative command with flags.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing flags for the field you need.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dry\">\u003cdt>Dry run output \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> YAML printed, nothing created.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Applying without reading.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"edit\">\u003cdt>Edit the file \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Add volumes, probes or security settings.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Tabs or wrong indentation.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>API server \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Validates and stores the object.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Immutable field changes rejected.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"chk\">\u003cdt>Verify \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> get, describe and logs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Assuming success.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-selector-that-did-not-match\">Incident: The selector that did not match\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A candidate hand-edited generated YAML and applied it; the deployment never created pods.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get deployment api\nkubectl describe deployment api\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deployment api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME   READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">api    0/3     0            0           2m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe deployment api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Selector:  app=api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Pod Template Labels:  app=backend\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The pod template labels had been changed to \u003ccode>app=backend\u003c/code> while the selector still said \u003ccode>app=api\u003c/code>, so the deployment could not claim its pods. Reading the describe output revealed the mismatch within seconds. Generating YAML and changing labels in only one place is a classic slip; check both.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Typing full YAML when an imperative command and dry run would do\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting \u003ccode>--dry-run=client -o yaml\u003c/code> and creating the object directly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mismatching selector and pod template labels\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Applying without reading generated YAML\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using latest tags in examples that then differ from the task&#39;s requirements\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"creating-objects-imperatively","text":"Creating objects imperatively"},{"id":"the-dry-run-pattern","text":"The dry-run pattern"},{"id":"changing-what-exists","text":"Changing what exists"},{"id":"reading-output-quickly","text":"Reading output quickly"},{"id":"working-inside-pods","text":"Working inside pods"},{"id":"know-your-limits","text":"Know your limits"},{"id":"a-quick-sanity-check","text":"A quick sanity check"},{"id":"incident-the-selector-that-did-not-match","text":"Incident: The selector that did not match"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"kubectl quick reference","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/quick-reference/"},{"t":"kubectl: JSONPath support","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/jsonpath/"}],"text":"Declarative YAML is the right way to manage clusters over time. In an exam, and in many incident situations, speed matters more. kubectl can create most common objects imperatively and print the YAML for you to adjust. Learning this set of commands is the best time investment for a hands-on test. What you will learn Imperative commands for the common objects The dry-run pattern for generating YAML Quick edits, patches and set commands Output formats that answer questions fast Creating objects imperatively kubectl run web --image=nginx:1.27 --port=80 --labels=app=web kubectl create deployment api --image=nginx:1.27 --replicas=3 kubectl expose deployment api --port=80 --target-port=8080 -- type =ClusterIP kubectl create configmap app-config --from-literal=mode=prod --from-file=settings.ini kubectl create secret generic db-pass --from-literal=password=changeme kubectl create job once --image=busybox:1.36 -- echo hello kubectl create cronjob tick --image=busybox:1.36 --schedule= \"*/5 * * * *\" -- date kubectl create namespace dev kubectl create serviceaccount deployer -n dev Anything after -- is the container command. Pin image tags, as in real life. The dry-run pattern When you need a field that no flag provides, generate YAML and edit it: kubectl create deployment api --image=nginx:1.27 --replicas=3 --dry-run=client -o yaml > api.yaml vim api.yaml kubectl apply -f api.yaml --dry-run=client prints the object without sending it to the server. With -o yaml you get a valid starting file. Add what you need, such as a volume or a security context, and apply. Use --dry-run=server when you want the API server&#39;s validation without creating anything. Changing what exists kubectl set image deployment/api nginx=nginx:1.27.1 kubectl set resources deployment/api --limits=cpu=500m,memory=256Mi --requests=cpu=100m,memory=128Mi kubectl scale deployment/api --replicas=5 kubectl label pod web tier=frontend kubectl annotate pod web owner=team-a kubectl rollout status deployment/api kubectl rollout undo deployment/api kubectl edit deployment api kubectl patch deployment api -p &#x27;{\"spec\":{\"replicas\":2}}&#x27; kubectl edit opens the live object in your editor; saving applies it. For fields that are immutable, such as most pod spec fields, you must delete and recreate, or use kubectl replace --force -f file.yaml . Reading output quickly kubectl get pods -o wide kubectl get pods --show-labels kubectl get pods -l app=web kubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase!=Running kubectl get pods --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp kubectl get pod web -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.status.podIP}&#x27; kubectl get nodes -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,CPU:.status.capacity.cpu kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp kubectl describe pod web kubectl logs web --previous kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.securityContext JSONPath and custom columns answer tasks such as \"write the names of all nodes to a file\" without manual copying. Redirect to a file with > and check it with cat afterwards. Working inside pods kubectl exec -it web -- sh kubectl run tmp -- rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- wget -qO- http://api kubectl cp web:/etc/nginx/nginx.conf ./nginx.conf kubectl port-forward svc/api 8080:80 The temporary pod pattern with --rm -it --restart=Never is the quickest way to test service names and network policies from inside the cluster. Know your limits Imperative commands cover the common cases. Pod volumes, init containers, probes, affinity, tolerations and most security settings need YAML. Know the dry-run pattern well enough that adding a block to generated YAML takes seconds. Keep a mental library of the three or four YAML snippets you use most, and copy the rest from the docs. A quick sanity check Before applying a generated file, read it once. Check the namespace, the name, labels matching selectors, and that no leftover fields such as status: {} or creationTimestamp: null cause problems. Those two are harmless, but a wrong namesp"},"k8s-exam/cka-cluster-setup-with-kubeadm-and-upgrades":{"html":"\u003cp>Cluster architecture, installation and configuration is about a quarter of the CKA. The tasks are procedural and well documented, which makes them good points for a prepared candidate. The Kubernetes module covers the concepts of the control plane and upgrades. This lesson turns them into the exact sequences you type, and what to check after each.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Preparing nodes and bootstrapping with kubeadm\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Joining workers and installing a pod network\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading a cluster by one minor version\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Draining, cordoning and basic high availability concepts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prepare-the-nodes\">Prepare the nodes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>kubeadm expects a Linux host with a container runtime, the kubelet, kubeadm and kubectl installed. Before initialising, the usual preparation is: swap disabled, required kernel modules loaded, sysctl values for bridged traffic and IP forwarding set, unique hostnames and MAC addresses, and open ports between nodes. A task may give you nodes that are partly prepared; check each of these rather than assuming.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"swapoff -a\nlsmod | grep br_netfilter\nsysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward\nsystemctl status containerd\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">swapoff -a\nlsmod | grep br_netfilter\nsysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward\nsystemctl status containerd\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"bootstrap-the-control-plane\">Bootstrap the control plane\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16 --kubernetes-version=v1.31.0\nmkdir -p $HOME/.kube\ncp -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config\nkubectl get nodes\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16 --kubernetes-version=v1.31.0\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mkdir\u003c/span> -p \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$HOME\u003c/span>/.kube\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cp\u003c/span> -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$HOME\u003c/span>/.kube/config\nkubectl get nodes\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The pod network range must match what your network plugin expects. After init, the node shows \u003ccode>NotReady\u003c/code> until a network plugin is installed. Apply the plugin&#39;s manifest following its documentation. Then check \u003ccode>kubectl get pods -n kube-system\u003c/code> until system pods are running.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"join-workers\">Join workers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The init output prints a join command with a token and a CA certificate hash. If it has expired or you lost it, create a new one on the control plane:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubeadm token create --print-join-command\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubeadm token create --print-join-command\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run the printed command as root on the worker. Then \u003ccode>kubectl get nodes\u003c/code> on the control plane should list it, and it becomes \u003ccode>Ready\u003c/code> once the network plugin is running there. For a highly available control plane, kubeadm supports additional control plane nodes behind a stable endpoint, using \u003ccode>--control-plane-endpoint\u003c/code> at init and \u003ccode>--control-plane\u003c/code> on join. Know the idea, and follow the docs for the exact flags.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"upgrade-one-minor-version\">Upgrade one minor version\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Kubernetes supports upgrading one minor version at a time. Never skip a minor version. The documented order is control plane first, then workers, node by node. On the first control plane node:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apt-get update\napt-cache madison kubeadm | head\napt-mark unhold kubeadm\napt-get install -y kubeadm='1.31.1-*'\napt-mark hold kubeadm\nkubeadm version\nkubeadm upgrade plan\nkubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.1\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">apt-get update\napt-cache madison kubeadm | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\napt-mark unhold kubeadm\napt-get install -y kubeadm=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;1.31.1-*&#x27;\u003c/span>\napt-mark hold kubeadm\nkubeadm version\nkubeadm upgrade plan\nkubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Then upgrade the kubelet and kubectl on that node, after draining it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl drain cp1 --ignore-daemonsets\napt-mark unhold kubelet kubectl\napt-get install -y kubelet='1.31.1-*' kubectl='1.31.1-*'\napt-mark hold kubelet kubectl\nsystemctl daemon-reload\nsystemctl restart kubelet\nkubectl uncordon cp1\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl drain cp1 --ignore-daemonsets\napt-mark unhold kubelet kubectl\napt-get install -y kubelet=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;1.31.1-*&#x27;\u003c/span> kubectl=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;1.31.1-*&#x27;\u003c/span>\napt-mark hold kubelet kubectl\nsystemctl daemon-reload\nsystemctl restart kubelet\nkubectl uncordon cp1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>On each worker, run \u003ccode>kubeadm upgrade node\u003c/code> after installing the new kubeadm, then drain, upgrade the kubelet, restart it and uncordon. Version numbers above are only an example; use the versions given in the task. On extra control plane nodes, use \u003ccode>kubeadm upgrade node\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>apply\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"drain-cordon-and-uncordon\">Drain, cordon and uncordon\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>kubectl cordon\u003c/code> marks a node unschedulable but leaves running pods. \u003ccode>kubectl drain\u003c/code> cordons and also evicts pods, respecting disruption budgets; it needs \u003ccode>--ignore-daemonsets\u003c/code> because daemon set pods cannot be evicted, and \u003ccode>--delete-emptydir-data\u003c/code> when pods use emptyDir volumes. \u003ccode>kubectl uncordon\u003c/code> makes the node schedulable again. Remember to uncordon after maintenance; leaving a node cordoned is a common lost point.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"other-configuration-tasks\">Other configuration tasks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The curriculum also covers managing role-based access (next lessons), using Helm and Kustomize to install cluster components, understanding extension interfaces such as CNI, CSI and CRI, and working with custom resource definitions and operators. Practise a basic Helm install and upgrade and a Kustomize overlay, because short tasks appear around them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami\nhelm install web bitnami/nginx -n web --create-namespace --set replicaCount=2\nhelm list -A\nkubectl kustomize ./overlays/prod\nkubectl apply -k ./overlays/prod\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami\nhelm install web bitnami/nginx -n web --create-namespace --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> replicaCount=2\nhelm list -A\nkubectl kustomize ./overlays/prod\nkubectl apply -k ./overlays/prod\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"check-what-you-did\">Check what you did\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>After any cluster change, verify: nodes Ready, system pods Running, and a test workload schedules and can reach a service. Certificates created by kubeadm expire after a year unless upgraded, and \u003ccode>kubeadm certs check-expiration\u003c/code> lists them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"nodes = {&quot;cp1&quot;: &quot;1.31.1&quot;, &quot;w1&quot;: &quot;1.31.0&quot;, &quot;w2&quot;: &quot;1.31.0&quot;}\ntarget = &quot;1.31.1&quot;\ntodo = [n for n, v in nodes.items() if v != target]\nprint(&quot;nodes still to upgrade:&quot;, todo)\n# upgrade order: control plane first, then workers one at a time\norder = [&quot;cp1&quot;, &quot;w1&quot;, &quot;w2&quot;]\nprint([n for n in order if n in todo])\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">nodes = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cp1&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;1.31.1&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;w1&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;1.31.0&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;w2&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;1.31.0&quot;\u003c/span>}\ntarget = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;1.31.1&quot;\u003c/span>\ntodo = [n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> n, v \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> nodes.items() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> v != target]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;nodes still to upgrade:&quot;\u003c/span>, todo)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># upgrade order: control plane first, then workers one at a time\u003c/span>\norder = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cp1&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;w1&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;w2&quot;\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>([n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> order \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> todo])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"plan&gt;cp;cp&gt;drain;drain&gt;wk;wk&gt;un;un&gt;chk\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Upgrading one minor version\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"90.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"plan\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">kubeadm upgrade plan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cp\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Control plane node\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"drain\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Drain a worker\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"wk\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:16.7%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Upgrade worker\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"un\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Uncordon\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"chk\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:83.3%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, kubeadm upgrade plan to Control plane node; 2, Control plane node to Drain a worker; 3, Drain a worker to Upgrade worker; 4, Upgrade worker to Uncordon; 5, Uncordon to Verify.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"plan\">\u003cdt>kubeadm upgrade plan \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shows available versions and checks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Planning a jump across minors.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cp\">\u003cdt>Control plane node \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> upgrade apply, then kubelet.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping the drain.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"drain\">\u003cdt>Drain a worker \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Evict pods, respect budgets.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forgetting ignore-daemonsets.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"wk\">\u003cdt>Upgrade worker \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> upgrade node, kubelet restart.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong package version.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"un\">\u003cdt>Uncordon \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Make it schedulable again.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Left cordoned.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"chk\">\u003cdt>Verify \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Nodes Ready, pods Running.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No post-check.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-worker-that-stayed-cordoned\">Incident: The worker that stayed cordoned\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After an upgrade task, a worker node stayed unschedulable, and a later task&#39;s pods stayed Pending.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get nodes\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME   STATUS                     ROLES           AGE   VERSION\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">cp1    Ready                      control-plane   30d   v1.31.1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">w1     Ready,SchedulingDisabled   &lt;none&gt;          30d   v1.31.1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The candidate had drained the worker, upgraded it, and forgot the final uncordon. Pods that needed to schedule had nowhere to go. The status text \u003ccode>SchedulingDisabled\u003c/code> is the clue. A habit of ending every maintenance sequence with \u003ccode>kubectl get nodes\u003c/code> catches it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Skipping a minor version during an upgrade\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting the daemon set flag when draining\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not uncordoning after maintenance\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using a join token that has expired\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Applying a network plugin whose pod range differs from the init setting\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"prepare-the-nodes","text":"Prepare the nodes"},{"id":"bootstrap-the-control-plane","text":"Bootstrap the control plane"},{"id":"join-workers","text":"Join workers"},{"id":"upgrade-one-minor-version","text":"Upgrade one minor version"},{"id":"drain-cordon-and-uncordon","text":"Drain, cordon and uncordon"},{"id":"other-configuration-tasks","text":"Other configuration tasks"},{"id":"check-what-you-did","text":"Check what you did"},{"id":"incident-the-worker-that-stayed-cordoned","text":"Incident: The worker that stayed cordoned"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: creating a cluster with kubeadm","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/setup/production-environment/tools/kubeadm/create-cluster-kubeadm/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: upgrading kubeadm clusters","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/kubeadm/kubeadm-upgrade/"}],"text":"Cluster architecture, installation and configuration is about a quarter of the CKA. The tasks are procedural and well documented, which makes them good points for a prepared candidate. The Kubernetes module covers the concepts of the control plane and upgrades. This lesson turns them into the exact sequences you type, and what to check after each. What you will learn Preparing nodes and bootstrapping with kubeadm Joining workers and installing a pod network Upgrading a cluster by one minor version Draining, cordoning and basic high availability concepts Prepare the nodes kubeadm expects a Linux host with a container runtime, the kubelet, kubeadm and kubectl installed. Before initialising, the usual preparation is: swap disabled, required kernel modules loaded, sysctl values for bridged traffic and IP forwarding set, unique hostnames and MAC addresses, and open ports between nodes. A task may give you nodes that are partly prepared; check each of these rather than assuming. swapoff -a lsmod | grep br_netfilter sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward systemctl status containerd Bootstrap the control plane kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16 --kubernetes-version=v1.31.0 mkdir -p $HOME /.kube cp -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME /.kube/config kubectl get nodes The pod network range must match what your network plugin expects. After init, the node shows NotReady until a network plugin is installed. Apply the plugin&#39;s manifest following its documentation. Then check kubectl get pods -n kube-system until system pods are running. Join workers The init output prints a join command with a token and a CA certificate hash. If it has expired or you lost it, create a new one on the control plane: kubeadm token create --print-join-command Run the printed command as root on the worker. Then kubectl get nodes on the control plane should list it, and it becomes Ready once the network plugin is running there. For a highly available control plane, kubeadm supports additional control plane nodes behind a stable endpoint, using --control-plane-endpoint at init and --control-plane on join. Know the idea, and follow the docs for the exact flags. Upgrade one minor version Kubernetes supports upgrading one minor version at a time. Never skip a minor version. The documented order is control plane first, then workers, node by node. On the first control plane node: apt-get update apt-cache madison kubeadm | head apt-mark unhold kubeadm apt-get install -y kubeadm= &#x27;1.31.1-*&#x27; apt-mark hold kubeadm kubeadm version kubeadm upgrade plan kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.1 Then upgrade the kubelet and kubectl on that node, after draining it: kubectl drain cp1 --ignore-daemonsets apt-mark unhold kubelet kubectl apt-get install -y kubelet= &#x27;1.31.1-*&#x27; kubectl= &#x27;1.31.1-*&#x27; apt-mark hold kubelet kubectl systemctl daemon-reload systemctl restart kubelet kubectl uncordon cp1 On each worker, run kubeadm upgrade node after installing the new kubeadm, then drain, upgrade the kubelet, restart it and uncordon. Version numbers above are only an example; use the versions given in the task. On extra control plane nodes, use kubeadm upgrade node instead of apply . Drain, cordon and uncordon kubectl cordon marks a node unschedulable but leaves running pods. kubectl drain cordons and also evicts pods, respecting disruption budgets; it needs --ignore-daemonsets because daemon set pods cannot be evicted, and --delete-emptydir-data when pods use emptyDir volumes. kubectl uncordon makes the node schedulable again. Remember to uncordon after maintenance; leaving a node cordoned is a common lost point. Other configuration tasks The curriculum also covers managing role-based access (next lessons), using Helm and Kustomize to install cluster components, understanding extension interfaces such as CNI, CSI and CRI, and working with custom resource definitions and operators. Practise a basic Helm install and upgrade and a Kustomize overlay, because short tasks app"},"k8s-exam/cka-etcd-backup-restore-and-static-pods":{"html":"\u003cp>etcd holds the cluster&#39;s state, so a backup and restore task is a classic. It is mechanical and has several exact parameters, which is why it rewards practice. The Kubernetes operations module explains why etcd matters. Here is the working sequence.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Finding the etcd endpoint and certificate paths\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Taking and verifying a snapshot\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restoring to a new directory and updating the manifest\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How static pods work and how to fix one\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"find-the-parameters\">Find the parameters\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>On a kubeadm cluster, etcd runs as a static pod. Its manifest is in the kubelet&#39;s static pod directory, usually \u003ccode>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml\u003c/code>. Read it to find the flags you need:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -E &quot;listen-client-urls|cert-file|key-file|trusted-ca-file|data-dir&quot; /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;listen-client-urls|cert-file|key-file|trusted-ca-file|data-dir&quot;\u003c/span> /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>You need the client endpoint, the CA certificate, the server certificate and its key, and the data directory. On a typical setup the certificates are under \u003ccode>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"take-a-snapshot\">Take a snapshot\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db \\\n  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \\\n  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \\\n  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \\\n  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db \\\n  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \\\n  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \\\n  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \\\n  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Check it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"etcdutl snapshot status /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db --write-out=table\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">etcdutl snapshot status /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db --write-out=table\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Newer etcd releases moved offline snapshot operations to \u003ccode>etcdutl\u003c/code>; older ones used \u003ccode>etcdctl snapshot status\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>restore\u003c/code>. Run \u003ccode>--help\u003c/code> to see which your environment provides. If \u003ccode>etcdctl\u003c/code> is not installed on the host, the task may expect you to run it from the etcd container or to install it. Read the task text for hints about where to store the file, and confirm the file exists afterwards.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"restore-to-a-new-directory\">Restore to a new directory\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Restoring does not overwrite the live data. It writes a new data directory, and you then point etcd at it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"etcdutl snapshot restore /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">etcdutl snapshot restore /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Edit the static pod manifest so the etcd data volume points to the new directory. In \u003ccode>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml\u003c/code>, find the \u003ccode>hostPath\u003c/code> for the volume named \u003ccode>etcd-data\u003c/code> and change \u003ccode>path\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>/var/lib/etcd-restored\u003c/code>. When you save the manifest, the kubelet notices the change and recreates the etcd pod. Wait a minute, then check:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"crictl ps | grep etcd\nkubectl get pods -n kube-system\nkubectl get nodes\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">crictl ps | grep etcd\nkubectl get pods -n kube-system\nkubectl get nodes\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>While etcd restarts, \u003ccode>kubectl\u003c/code> will fail briefly because the API server cannot reach its store. That is normal. If it does not recover, look at the etcd container logs with \u003ccode>crictl logs\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"static-pods\">Static pods\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A static pod is managed directly by the kubelet from a manifest file, not through the API server. The kubelet watches a directory, set by \u003ccode>staticPodPath\u003c/code> in its configuration file, usually \u003ccode>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml\u003c/code>. Control plane components on a kubeadm cluster are static pods: etcd, kube-apiserver, kube-controller-manager and kube-scheduler.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Consequences worth knowing:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Editing the file changes the pod; the kubelet restarts it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Removing the file removes the pod\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The API server shows a read-only &quot;mirror&quot; copy of the pod, named with the node name suffix\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>You cannot delete a static pod with \u003ccode>kubectl delete\u003c/code>; it comes back. Remove or move its manifest file instead\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Tasks may ask you to create a static pod on a worker. Find that node&#39;s \u003ccode>staticPodPath\u003c/code> and write a pod manifest there, which you can generate with a dry run.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl run static-web --image=nginx:1.27 --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; /etc/kubernetes/manifests/static-web.yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl run static-web --image=nginx:1.27 --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; /etc/kubernetes/manifests/static-web.yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"check-what-you-changed\">Check what you changed\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>After a restore, verify that the cluster objects you expect are there, such as a namespace or deployment that existed at snapshot time. If the task asks you to restore a specific snapshot file, make sure you used that file and not a different one.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# a simple pre-restore check of the values you will need\nrequired = [&quot;endpoints&quot;, &quot;cacert&quot;, &quot;cert&quot;, &quot;key&quot;, &quot;snapshot-path&quot;, &quot;new-data-dir&quot;]\ngiven = {&quot;endpoints&quot;: &quot;https://127.0.0.1:2379&quot;, &quot;cacert&quot;: &quot;/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt&quot;,\n         &quot;cert&quot;: &quot;/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt&quot;, &quot;key&quot;: &quot;/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key&quot;,\n         &quot;snapshot-path&quot;: &quot;/opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db&quot;}\nprint(&quot;still needed:&quot;, [k for k in required if k not in given])\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># a simple pre-restore check of the values you will need\u003c/span>\nrequired = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;endpoints&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cacert&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cert&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;key&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;snapshot-path&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;new-data-dir&quot;\u003c/span>]\ngiven = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;endpoints&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;https://127.0.0.1:2379&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cacert&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt&quot;\u003c/span>,\n         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cert&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;key&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key&quot;\u003c/span>,\n         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;snapshot-path&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db&quot;\u003c/span>}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;still needed:&quot;\u003c/span>, [k \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> k \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> required \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> k \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> given])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"4\" data-edges=\"man&gt;snap;snap&gt;file;file&gt;rest;rest&gt;edit;edit&gt;kub\" style=\"--rows:4\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Backup and restore of etcd\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"90.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"87.5\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"man\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">etcd manifest\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"snap\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Snapshot save\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"file\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Snapshot file\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rest\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Restore\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"edit\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Edit manifest\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"kub\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:87.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">kubelet recreates\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, etcd manifest to Snapshot save; 2, Snapshot save to Snapshot file; 3, Snapshot file to Restore; 4, Restore to Edit manifest; 5, Edit manifest to kubelet recreates.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"man\">\u003cdt>etcd manifest \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Endpoint, certificates and data directory.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Reading the wrong certificate path.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"snap\">\u003cdt>Snapshot save \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> etcdctl with endpoint and certificates.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing certificate flags.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"file\">\u003cdt>Snapshot file \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Verified with snapshot status.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not checked, saved to wrong path.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rest\">\u003cdt>Restore \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> New data directory from the snapshot.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Overwriting live data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"edit\">\u003cdt>Edit manifest \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Point etcd-data at the new directory.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Changing the wrong volume.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"kub\">\u003cdt>kubelet recreates \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Static pod restarts with restored data.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Typos stop the pod starting.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-snapshot-that-was-never-saved\">Incident: The snapshot that was never saved\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A candidate ran the backup command, saw no error and moved on. Later the restore task failed because the snapshot file was empty.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup/etcd.db --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379\nls -l /opt/backup/etcd.db\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup/etcd.db --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error: context deadline exceeded\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -l /opt/backup/etcd.db\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-rw------- 1 root root 0 Nov 14 08:40 /opt/backup/etcd.db\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The command had been run without the certificate flags, so it hung and timed out, leaving an empty file. The error had scrolled by. Always check the exit status or the file size, and run \u003ccode>snapshot status\u003c/code> on the result. A snapshot you have not verified is not a backup.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Omitting one of the four connection flags\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not verifying the snapshot after saving it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restoring into the live data directory\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing the wrong volume in the etcd manifest\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trying to delete a static pod with kubectl instead of removing its manifest\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"find-the-parameters","text":"Find the parameters"},{"id":"take-a-snapshot","text":"Take a snapshot"},{"id":"restore-to-a-new-directory","text":"Restore to a new directory"},{"id":"static-pods","text":"Static pods"},{"id":"check-what-you-changed","text":"Check what you changed"},{"id":"incident-the-snapshot-that-was-never-saved","text":"Incident: The snapshot that was never saved"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: operating etcd clusters","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/configure-upgrade-etcd/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: static pods","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/static-pod/"}],"text":"etcd holds the cluster&#39;s state, so a backup and restore task is a classic. It is mechanical and has several exact parameters, which is why it rewards practice. The Kubernetes operations module explains why etcd matters. Here is the working sequence. What you will learn Finding the etcd endpoint and certificate paths Taking and verifying a snapshot Restoring to a new directory and updating the manifest How static pods work and how to fix one Find the parameters On a kubeadm cluster, etcd runs as a static pod. Its manifest is in the kubelet&#39;s static pod directory, usually /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml . Read it to find the flags you need: grep -E \"listen-client-urls|cert-file|key-file|trusted-ca-file|data-dir\" /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml You need the client endpoint, the CA certificate, the server certificate and its key, and the data directory. On a typical setup the certificates are under /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ . Take a snapshot ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db \\ --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \\ --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \\ --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \\ --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key Check it: etcdutl snapshot status /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db --write-out=table Newer etcd releases moved offline snapshot operations to etcdutl ; older ones used etcdctl snapshot status and restore . Run --help to see which your environment provides. If etcdctl is not installed on the host, the task may expect you to run it from the etcd container or to install it. Read the task text for hints about where to store the file, and confirm the file exists afterwards. Restore to a new directory Restoring does not overwrite the live data. It writes a new data directory, and you then point etcd at it. etcdutl snapshot restore /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored Edit the static pod manifest so the etcd data volume points to the new directory. In /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml , find the hostPath for the volume named etcd-data and change path to /var/lib/etcd-restored . When you save the manifest, the kubelet notices the change and recreates the etcd pod. Wait a minute, then check: crictl ps | grep etcd kubectl get pods -n kube-system kubectl get nodes While etcd restarts, kubectl will fail briefly because the API server cannot reach its store. That is normal. If it does not recover, look at the etcd container logs with crictl logs . Static pods A static pod is managed directly by the kubelet from a manifest file, not through the API server. The kubelet watches a directory, set by staticPodPath in its configuration file, usually /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml . Control plane components on a kubeadm cluster are static pods: etcd, kube-apiserver, kube-controller-manager and kube-scheduler. Consequences worth knowing: Editing the file changes the pod; the kubelet restarts it Removing the file removes the pod The API server shows a read-only \"mirror\" copy of the pod, named with the node name suffix You cannot delete a static pod with kubectl delete ; it comes back. Remove or move its manifest file instead Tasks may ask you to create a static pod on a worker. Find that node&#39;s staticPodPath and write a pod manifest there, which you can generate with a dry run. kubectl run static-web --image=nginx:1.27 --dry-run=client -o yaml > /etc/kubernetes/manifests/static-web.yaml Check what you changed After a restore, verify that the cluster objects you expect are there, such as a namespace or deployment that existed at snapshot time. If the task asks you to restore a specific snapshot file, make sure you used that file and not a different one. # a simple pre-restore check of the values you will need required = [ \"endpoints\" , \"cacert\" , \"cert\" , \"key\" , \"snapshot-path\" , \"new-data-dir\" ] given = { \"endpoints\" : \"https://127.0.0.1:2379\" , \"cacert\" : \"/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt\" , \"cert\" : \"/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt\" , \"key"},"k8s-exam/cka-rbac-kubeconfig-and-certificate-tasks":{"html":"\u003cp>Access control questions are quick to solve when you know the commands, and slow when you build the YAML from scratch. This lesson gives you the imperative forms, the checks and the certificate flow for creating a user.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Roles, cluster roles and bindings from the command line\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Verifying access with auth can-i\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Service accounts and their tokens\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating a user certificate with the CSR API and kubeconfig\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"roles-and-bindings\">Roles and bindings\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A Role grants permissions inside one namespace. A ClusterRole grants cluster-wide permissions, or permissions on cluster-scoped resources. A RoleBinding grants a Role (or a ClusterRole, within one namespace) to subjects; a ClusterRoleBinding grants cluster-wide.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl create role pod-reader --verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods -n dev\nkubectl create rolebinding read-pods --role=pod-reader --user=jane -n dev\nkubectl create clusterrole node-viewer --verb=get,list --resource=nodes\nkubectl create clusterrolebinding view-nodes --clusterrole=node-viewer --user=jane\nkubectl create rolebinding deployer --clusterrole=edit --serviceaccount=dev:deployer -n dev\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl create role pod-reader --verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods -n dev\nkubectl create rolebinding read-pods --role=pod-reader --user=jane -n dev\nkubectl create clusterrole node-viewer --verb=get,list --resource=nodes\nkubectl create clusterrolebinding view-nodes --clusterrole=node-viewer --user=jane\nkubectl create rolebinding deployer --clusterrole=edit --serviceaccount=dev:deployer -n dev\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Note the service account form, \u003ccode>namespace:name\u003c/code>. Reusing a built-in ClusterRole such as \u003ccode>view\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>edit\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>admin\u003c/code> through a RoleBinding limits it to one namespace and saves writing rules.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"test-before-you-trust\">Test before you trust\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl auth can-i list pods -n dev --as jane\nkubectl auth can-i delete pods -n dev --as jane\nkubectl auth can-i get nodes --as jane\nkubectl auth can-i --list -n dev --as jane\nkubectl auth can-i create deployments -n dev --as system:serviceaccount:dev:deployer\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl auth can-i list pods -n dev --as jane\nkubectl auth can-i delete pods -n dev --as jane\nkubectl auth can-i get nodes --as jane\nkubectl auth can-i --list -n dev --as jane\nkubectl auth can-i create deployments -n dev --as system:serviceaccount:dev:deployer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>--as\u003c/code> option checks permissions for another identity, which needs your own account to be allowed to impersonate. Use \u003ccode>yes\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>no\u003c/code> outputs as your verification. If a task says &quot;allow user X to do Y&quot;, confirm both the allowed action and one that should be denied.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"aggregated-rules-and-subresources\">Aggregated rules and subresources\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Rules can name subresources such as \u003ccode>pods/log\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>deployments/scale\u003c/code>, and specific resource names through \u003ccode>--resource-name\u003c/code>. When a task needs &quot;read logs of pods&quot; you need \u003ccode>pods/log\u003c/code>, not only \u003ccode>pods\u003c/code>. If you are unsure, use \u003ccode>kubectl api-resources\u003c/code> to see resource names and API groups, and note that group names matter for the \u003ccode>apiGroups\u003c/code> field in YAML: core resources use an empty group, deployments use \u003ccode>apps\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"service-accounts\">Service accounts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl create serviceaccount deployer -n dev\nkubectl create token deployer -n dev\nkubectl run tool --image=busybox:1.36 --overrides='{&quot;spec&quot;:{&quot;serviceAccountName&quot;:&quot;deployer&quot;}}' -n dev --dry-run=client -o yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl create serviceaccount deployer -n dev\nkubectl create token deployer -n dev\nkubectl run tool --image=busybox:1.36 --overrides=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;spec&quot;:{&quot;serviceAccountName&quot;:&quot;deployer&quot;}}&#x27;\u003c/span> -n dev --dry-run=client -o yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Since Kubernetes 1.24, service accounts no longer get long-lived token secrets automatically, and \u003ccode>kubectl create token\u003c/code> issues a short-lived token. In a pod, set \u003ccode>serviceAccountName\u003c/code> in the spec to use an account. To stop a pod from receiving a token it does not need, set \u003ccode>automountServiceAccountToken: false\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"create-a-user-with-the-csr-api\">Create a user with the CSR API\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Kubernetes has no user objects; users are identities proven by certificates or other authentication. To add a user with a client certificate:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openssl genrsa -out jane.key 2048\nopenssl req -new -key jane.key -subj &quot;/CN=jane/O=dev-team&quot; -out jane.csr\nbase64 -w0 jane.csr\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">openssl genrsa -out jane.key 2048\nopenssl req -new -key jane.key -subj \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/CN=jane/O=dev-team&quot;\u003c/span> -out jane.csr\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">base64\u003c/span> -w0 jane.csr\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Create a CertificateSigningRequest object with that content:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: certificates.k8s.io/v1\nkind: CertificateSigningRequest\nmetadata:\n  name: jane\nspec:\n  request: LS0tLS1CRUdJTi...   # base64 of jane.csr\n  signerName: kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client\n  usages:\n    - client auth\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">certificates.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">CertificateSigningRequest\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">jane\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">request:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">LS0tLS1CRUdJTi...\u003c/span>   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># base64 of jane.csr\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">signerName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">usages:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">client\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">auth\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Then approve it and fetch the certificate:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl certificate approve jane\nkubectl get csr jane -o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' | base64 -d &gt; jane.crt\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl certificate approve jane\nkubectl get csr jane -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.status.certificate}&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">base64\u003c/span> -d &gt; jane.crt\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The CN becomes the user name and O becomes a group. Grant permissions with a binding to the user or group.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"build-a-kubeconfig-entry\">Build a kubeconfig entry\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl config set-credentials jane --client-certificate=jane.crt --client-key=jane.key --embed-certs=true\nkubectl config set-context jane@kubernetes --cluster=kubernetes --user=jane --namespace=dev\nkubectl config use-context jane@kubernetes\nkubectl config view --minify\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl config set-credentials jane --client-certificate=jane.crt --client-key=jane.key --embed-certs=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\nkubectl config set-context jane@kubernetes --cluster=kubernetes --user=jane --namespace=dev\nkubectl config use-context jane@kubernetes\nkubectl config view --minify\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>When a task says to write a kubeconfig to a given file, use \u003ccode>--kubeconfig=/path\u003c/code> consistently on each command, or set \u003ccode>KUBECONFIG\u003c/code>. Check the result with \u003ccode>kubectl --kubeconfig=/path config view\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"check-bindings-you-did-not-write\">Check bindings you did not write\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Sometimes the task is to find who can do something dangerous or why a service account is denied. Use \u003ccode>kubectl get rolebinding,clusterrolebinding -A -o wide\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>kubectl describe\u003c/code> a binding to see the role and subjects. Remember that permissions only add up: there are no deny rules.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"rules = {\n    &quot;pod-reader&quot;: {&quot;pods&quot;: {&quot;get&quot;, &quot;list&quot;, &quot;watch&quot;}},\n    &quot;editor&quot;: {&quot;pods&quot;: {&quot;get&quot;, &quot;list&quot;, &quot;create&quot;, &quot;delete&quot;}, &quot;deployments&quot;: {&quot;get&quot;, &quot;list&quot;, &quot;create&quot;}},\n}\nbindings = {&quot;jane&quot;: [&quot;pod-reader&quot;], &quot;deployer&quot;: [&quot;editor&quot;]}\n\ndef can(user, verb, resource):\n    return any(verb in rules[r].get(resource, set()) for r in bindings.get(user, []))\n\nprint(can(&quot;jane&quot;, &quot;list&quot;, &quot;pods&quot;), can(&quot;jane&quot;, &quot;delete&quot;, &quot;pods&quot;), can(&quot;deployer&quot;, &quot;create&quot;, &quot;deployments&quot;))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">rules = {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pod-reader&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pods&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;get&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;list&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;watch&quot;\u003c/span>}},\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;editor&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pods&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;get&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;list&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;create&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;delete&quot;\u003c/span>}, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;deployments&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;get&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;list&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;create&quot;\u003c/span>}},\n}\nbindings = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;jane&quot;\u003c/span>: [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pod-reader&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;deployer&quot;\u003c/span>: [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;editor&quot;\u003c/span>]}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">can\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">user, verb, resource\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">any\u003c/span>(verb \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> rules[r].get(resource, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span>()) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> r \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> bindings.get(user, []))\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(can(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;jane&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;list&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pods&quot;\u003c/span>), can(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;jane&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;delete&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pods&quot;\u003c/span>), can(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;deployer&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;create&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;deployments&quot;\u003c/span>))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"4\" data-edges=\"key&gt;csr;csr&gt;apr;apr&gt;cfg;cfg&gt;rbac;rbac&gt;test\" style=\"--rows:4\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Giving a user access\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"90.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"87.5\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"key\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Key and CSR\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"csr\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CertificateSigningRequest\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"apr\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Approve and fetch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cfg\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">kubeconfig entry\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rbac\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Role and binding\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"test\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:87.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">auth can-i\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Key and CSR to CertificateSigningRequest; 2, CertificateSigningRequest to Approve and fetch; 3, Approve and fetch to kubeconfig entry; 4, kubeconfig entry to Role and binding; 5, Role and binding to auth can-i.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"key\">\u003cdt>Key and CSR \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> openssl with CN and O.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong common name.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"csr\">\u003cdt>CertificateSigningRequest \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Submitted to the API.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong signer name.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"apr\">\u003cdt>Approve and fetch \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> kubectl certificate approve.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forgetting to approve.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cfg\">\u003cdt>kubeconfig entry \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Credentials and context.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong cluster name.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rbac\">\u003cdt>Role and binding \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> What the identity may do.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Binding to a different name.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"test\">\u003cdt>auth can-i \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Prove allowed and denied.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Testing only the allowed case.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-allowed-to-list-not-to-read-logs\">Incident: Allowed to list, not to read logs\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A user with a pod reader role could list pods but got an error when reading logs for a task.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl auth can-i get pods/log -n dev --as jane\nkubectl logs web -n dev --as jane\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl auth can-i get pods/log -n dev --as jane\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">no\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs web -n dev --as jane\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error from server (Forbidden): pods &quot;web&quot; is forbidden: User &quot;jane&quot; cannot get resource &quot;pods/log&quot; in API group &quot;&quot; in the namespace &quot;dev&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The role named only \u003ccode>pods\u003c/code>, but logs are a separate subresource, \u003ccode>pods/log\u003c/code>. Adding it to the role&#39;s resources fixed the problem. The error message names the exact resource, verb and namespace, which is the quickest guide to what to add.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Forgetting subresources such as pods/log or pods/exec\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing the service account subject without its namespace\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating a CSR with the wrong signer name\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Granting a ClusterRoleBinding when a RoleBinding was enough\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing only that access is allowed, not that other access is denied\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"roles-and-bindings","text":"Roles and bindings"},{"id":"test-before-you-trust","text":"Test before you trust"},{"id":"aggregated-rules-and-subresources","text":"Aggregated rules and subresources"},{"id":"service-accounts","text":"Service accounts"},{"id":"create-a-user-with-the-csr-api","text":"Create a user with the CSR API"},{"id":"build-a-kubeconfig-entry","text":"Build a kubeconfig entry"},{"id":"check-bindings-you-did-not-write","text":"Check bindings you did not write"},{"id":"incident-allowed-to-list-not-to-read-logs","text":"Incident: Allowed to list, not to read logs"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: RBAC authorization","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/rbac/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: certificates and certificate signing requests","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/certificate-signing-requests/"}],"text":"Access control questions are quick to solve when you know the commands, and slow when you build the YAML from scratch. This lesson gives you the imperative forms, the checks and the certificate flow for creating a user. What you will learn Roles, cluster roles and bindings from the command line Verifying access with auth can-i Service accounts and their tokens Creating a user certificate with the CSR API and kubeconfig Roles and bindings A Role grants permissions inside one namespace. A ClusterRole grants cluster-wide permissions, or permissions on cluster-scoped resources. A RoleBinding grants a Role (or a ClusterRole, within one namespace) to subjects; a ClusterRoleBinding grants cluster-wide. kubectl create role pod-reader --verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods -n dev kubectl create rolebinding read-pods --role=pod-reader --user=jane -n dev kubectl create clusterrole node-viewer --verb=get,list --resource=nodes kubectl create clusterrolebinding view-nodes --clusterrole=node-viewer --user=jane kubectl create rolebinding deployer --clusterrole=edit --serviceaccount=dev:deployer -n dev Note the service account form, namespace:name . Reusing a built-in ClusterRole such as view , edit or admin through a RoleBinding limits it to one namespace and saves writing rules. Test before you trust kubectl auth can-i list pods -n dev --as jane kubectl auth can-i delete pods -n dev --as jane kubectl auth can-i get nodes --as jane kubectl auth can-i --list -n dev --as jane kubectl auth can-i create deployments -n dev --as system:serviceaccount:dev:deployer The --as option checks permissions for another identity, which needs your own account to be allowed to impersonate. Use yes and no outputs as your verification. If a task says \"allow user X to do Y\", confirm both the allowed action and one that should be denied. Aggregated rules and subresources Rules can name subresources such as pods/log or deployments/scale , and specific resource names through --resource-name . When a task needs \"read logs of pods\" you need pods/log , not only pods . If you are unsure, use kubectl api-resources to see resource names and API groups, and note that group names matter for the apiGroups field in YAML: core resources use an empty group, deployments use apps . Service accounts kubectl create serviceaccount deployer -n dev kubectl create token deployer -n dev kubectl run tool --image=busybox:1.36 --overrides= &#x27;{\"spec\":{\"serviceAccountName\":\"deployer\"}}&#x27; -n dev --dry-run=client -o yaml Since Kubernetes 1.24, service accounts no longer get long-lived token secrets automatically, and kubectl create token issues a short-lived token. In a pod, set serviceAccountName in the spec to use an account. To stop a pod from receiving a token it does not need, set automountServiceAccountToken: false . Create a user with the CSR API Kubernetes has no user objects; users are identities proven by certificates or other authentication. To add a user with a client certificate: openssl genrsa -out jane.key 2048 openssl req -new -key jane.key -subj \"/CN=jane/O=dev-team\" -out jane.csr base64 -w0 jane.csr Create a CertificateSigningRequest object with that content: apiVersion: certificates.k8s.io/v1 kind: CertificateSigningRequest metadata: name: jane spec: request: LS0tLS1CRUdJTi... # base64 of jane.csr signerName: kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client usages: - client auth Then approve it and fetch the certificate: kubectl certificate approve jane kubectl get csr jane -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.status.certificate}&#x27; | base64 -d > jane.crt The CN becomes the user name and O becomes a group. Grant permissions with a binding to the user or group. Build a kubeconfig entry kubectl config set-credentials jane --client-certificate=jane.crt --client-key=jane.key --embed-certs= true kubectl config set-context jane@kubernetes --cluster=kubernetes --user=jane --namespace=dev kubectl config use-context jane@kubernetes kubectl config view --minify When a task says to write a kubeconfig to a"},"k8s-exam/cka-workloads-scheduling-and-configuration-tasks":{"html":"\u003cp>Workloads and scheduling is about fifteen percent of the CKA, but the same skills appear inside other tasks, so they pay off widely. Most of these tasks combine a short imperative command with one block of YAML that you copy from the docs. The goal here is to know which block you need and where to put it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Rollouts, rollbacks and scaling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>ConfigMaps and Secrets used as environment variables and volumes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Requests, limits and autoscaling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Placement: node selectors, affinity, taints and tolerations\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"deployments-rollouts-and-rollbacks\">Deployments, rollouts and rollbacks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl create deployment web --image=nginx:1.26 --replicas=3\nkubectl set image deployment/web nginx=nginx:1.27\nkubectl rollout status deployment/web\nkubectl rollout history deployment/web\nkubectl rollout undo deployment/web\nkubectl rollout undo deployment/web --to-revision=1\nkubectl scale deployment/web --replicas=5\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl create deployment web --image=nginx:1.26 --replicas=3\nkubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> image deployment/web nginx=nginx:1.27\nkubectl rollout status deployment/web\nkubectl rollout \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> deployment/web\nkubectl rollout undo deployment/web\nkubectl rollout undo deployment/web --to-revision=1\nkubectl scale deployment/web --replicas=5\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The container name for \u003ccode>set image\u003c/code> is the name inside the pod template, which for \u003ccode>kubectl create deployment\u003c/code> equals the image base name. If unsure, \u003ccode>kubectl get deployment web -o jsonpath=&#39;{.spec.template.spec.containers[*].name}&#39;\u003c/code> tells you. The rolling update strategy, with \u003ccode>maxSurge\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>maxUnavailable\u003c/code>, is set under \u003ccode>spec.strategy\u003c/code>; know where it lives.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"configmaps-and-secrets\">ConfigMaps and Secrets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl create configmap app --from-literal=LOG_LEVEL=debug\nkubectl create secret generic db --from-literal=password=s3cret\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl create configmap app --from-literal=LOG_LEVEL=debug\nkubectl create secret generic db --from-literal=password=s3cret\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use them in a pod as environment variables or files:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"spec:\n  containers:\n    - name: app\n      image: nginx:1.27\n      env:\n        - name: LOG_LEVEL\n          valueFrom:\n            configMapKeyRef:\n              name: app\n              key: LOG_LEVEL\n      envFrom:\n        - secretRef:\n            name: db\n      volumeMounts:\n        - name: cfg\n          mountPath: /etc/app\n  volumes:\n    - name: cfg\n      configMap:\n        name: app\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">app\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx:1.27\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">env:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">LOG_LEVEL\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">valueFrom:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">configMapKeyRef:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">app\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">key:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">LOG_LEVEL\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">envFrom:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">secretRef:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">db\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumeMounts:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">cfg\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mountPath:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/etc/app\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumes:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">cfg\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">configMap:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">app\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Secrets are base64 encoded, not encrypted; \u003ccode>kubectl get secret db -o jsonpath=&#39;{.data.password}&#39; | base64 -d\u003c/code> decodes one. Mounting a ConfigMap as a volume creates a file per key, and mounted files update after a while when the ConfigMap changes, while environment variables do not.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"resources-and-quotas\">Resources and quotas\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Requests are what the scheduler reserves; limits are the ceiling at runtime. A container that exceeds its memory limit is killed with an out-of-memory status, and one that exceeds its CPU limit is throttled.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"resources:\n  requests:\n    cpu: 100m\n    memory: 128Mi\n  limits:\n    cpu: 500m\n    memory: 256Mi\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">requests:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cpu:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">100m\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">128Mi\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">limits:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cpu:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">500m\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">256Mi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A LimitRange sets default and maximum values per namespace, and a ResourceQuota caps the total. A task that says &quot;pods in this namespace must not exceed X&quot; usually means one of these. A pod that asks for more than any node can offer stays Pending, with an event that says insufficient CPU or memory.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"autoscaling\">Autoscaling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A HorizontalPodAutoscaler needs metrics, normally from the metrics server, and CPU requests set on the target pods. A compact definition:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: autoscaling/v2\nkind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler\nmetadata:\n  name: web\nspec:\n  scaleTargetRef:\n    apiVersion: apps/v1\n    kind: Deployment\n    name: web\n  minReplicas: 2\n  maxReplicas: 6\n  metrics:\n    - type: Resource\n      resource:\n        name: cpu\n        target:\n          type: Utilization\n          averageUtilization: 60\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">autoscaling/v2\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">HorizontalPodAutoscaler\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">scaleTargetRef:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">apps/v1\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Deployment\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">minReplicas:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">maxReplicas:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">6\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metrics:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Resource\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resource:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">cpu\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">target:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Utilization\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">averageUtilization:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">60\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Check with \u003ccode>kubectl get hpa\u003c/code>. If the target shows \u003ccode>&lt;unknown&gt;\u003c/code>, metrics are missing or the pods have no CPU request.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"placing-pods\">Placing pods\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Four tools, from simplest to most flexible:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>nodeSelector\u003c/strong>: match a label on the node. \u003ccode>kubectl label node w1 disk=ssd\u003c/code>, then \u003ccode>nodeSelector: {disk: ssd}\u003c/code> in the pod spec.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Node affinity\u003c/strong>: required or preferred rules with operators; more expressive than a selector.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Taints and tolerations\u003c/strong>: a taint repels pods from a node, and a toleration lets a pod ignore it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Pod affinity and anti-affinity\u003c/strong>: place pods near or away from other pods, using a topology key such as \u003ccode>kubernetes.io/hostname\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl taint nodes w1 dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule\nkubectl taint nodes w1 dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule-\nkubectl describe node w1 | grep -i taint\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl taint nodes w1 dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule\nkubectl taint nodes w1 dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule-\nkubectl describe node w1 | grep -i taint\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The trailing minus removes the taint. A toleration matching it looks like:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"tolerations:\n  - key: dedicated\n    operator: Equal\n    value: gpu\n    effect: NoSchedule\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">tolerations:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">key:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">dedicated\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">operator:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Equal\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">value:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">gpu\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">effect:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">NoSchedule\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A toleration allows scheduling on a tainted node; it does not require it. To force pods onto those nodes, combine a toleration with a node selector or affinity.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"probes-init-containers-and-disruption\">Probes, init containers and disruption\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Liveness probes restart stuck containers, readiness probes control service traffic, and startup probes protect slow starters. Init containers run to completion before app containers, which suits waiting for a dependency. A PodDisruptionBudget limits voluntary evictions, such as during a drain; a budget that requires all replicas to be available blocks the drain forever.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"verify-placement\">Verify placement\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>After creating a pod, \u003ccode>kubectl get pod -o wide\u003c/code> shows its node. If it is Pending, \u003ccode>kubectl describe pod\u003c/code> lists the scheduler&#39;s reason, which names taints, selectors or resources.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"nodes = {&quot;w1&quot;: {&quot;taints&quot;: {&quot;dedicated=gpu&quot;}, &quot;labels&quot;: {&quot;disk&quot;: &quot;ssd&quot;}},\n         &quot;w2&quot;: {&quot;taints&quot;: set(), &quot;labels&quot;: {&quot;disk&quot;: &quot;hdd&quot;}}}\npod = {&quot;tolerations&quot;: set(), &quot;selector&quot;: {&quot;disk&quot;: &quot;ssd&quot;}}\n\ndef fits(node):\n    if not node[&quot;taints&quot;] &lt;= pod[&quot;tolerations&quot;]:\n        return &quot;tainted&quot;\n    if any(node[&quot;labels&quot;].get(k) != v for k, v in pod[&quot;selector&quot;].items()):\n        return &quot;selector mismatch&quot;\n    return &quot;ok&quot;\n\nprint({n: fits(v) for n, v in nodes.items()})\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">nodes = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;w1&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;taints&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dedicated=gpu&quot;\u003c/span>}, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;labels&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;disk&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ssd&quot;\u003c/span>}},\n         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;w2&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;taints&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span>(), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;labels&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;disk&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;hdd&quot;\u003c/span>}}}\npod = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tolerations&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span>(), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;selector&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;disk&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ssd&quot;\u003c/span>}}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">fits\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">node\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> node[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;taints&quot;\u003c/span>] &lt;= pod[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tolerations&quot;\u003c/span>]:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tainted&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">any\u003c/span>(node[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;labels&quot;\u003c/span>].get(k) != v \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> k, v \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> pod[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;selector&quot;\u003c/span>].items()):\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;selector mismatch&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ok&quot;\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>({n: fits(v) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> n, v \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> nodes.items()})\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"4\" data-edges=\"pend&gt;res;res&gt;tnt;res&gt;sel;tnt&gt;sch;sel&gt;sch;sch&gt;run\" style=\"--rows:4\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How a pod finds a node\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"12.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"12.5\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"12.5\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"87.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"87.5\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pend\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pending pod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"res\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:12.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Resource fit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tnt\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Taints and tolerations\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sel\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:87.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Selectors and affinity\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sch\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Scheduler decision\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"run\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Running pod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pending pod to Resource fit; 2, Resource fit to Taints and tolerations; 3, Resource fit to Selectors and affinity; 4, Taints and tolerations to Scheduler decision; 5, Selectors and affinity to Scheduler decision; 6, Scheduler decision to Running pod.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pend\">\u003cdt>Pending pod \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Needs a node that fits.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No node qualifies.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"res\">\u003cdt>Resource fit \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Requests against free capacity.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Requests larger than any node.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tnt\">\u003cdt>Taints and tolerations \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tainted nodes repel unless tolerated.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Toleration missing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sel\">\u003cdt>Selectors and affinity \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Labels the pod asks for.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Label typo on the node.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sch\">\u003cdt>Scheduler decision \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Chooses among the nodes left.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Events show why none fit.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"run\">\u003cdt>Running pod \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Bound to a node.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Verify with get -o wide.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-pending-because-of-a-taint\">Incident: Pending because of a taint\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A pod stayed Pending, and the candidate kept changing resource limits instead of reading the event.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe pod batch-1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pod batch-1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Events:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  Warning  FailedScheduling  0/3 nodes are available: 1 node(s) had untolerated taint {dedicated: gpu}, 2 node(s) didn't match Pod's node affinity/selector.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The event names both causes: one node was tainted and the others did not match the node selector. Reading the events line showed that the selector pointed to a label that did not exist on any untainted node. The fix was to correct the label, not to adjust resources. The scheduler&#39;s message is nearly always the shortest route to the cause.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using the wrong container name in kubectl set image\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting a toleration to force pods onto a node\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting CPU target in an HPA but no CPU request on the pods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A PodDisruptionBudget that blocks draining\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing resources when the event points to a taint or selector\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"deployments-rollouts-and-rollbacks","text":"Deployments, rollouts and rollbacks"},{"id":"configmaps-and-secrets","text":"ConfigMaps and Secrets"},{"id":"resources-and-quotas","text":"Resources and quotas"},{"id":"autoscaling","text":"Autoscaling"},{"id":"placing-pods","text":"Placing pods"},{"id":"probes-init-containers-and-disruption","text":"Probes, init containers and disruption"},{"id":"verify-placement","text":"Verify placement"},{"id":"incident-pending-because-of-a-taint","text":"Incident: Pending because of a taint"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: taints and tolerations","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/taint-and-toleration/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: assigning pods to nodes","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/assign-pod-node/"}],"text":"Workloads and scheduling is about fifteen percent of the CKA, but the same skills appear inside other tasks, so they pay off widely. Most of these tasks combine a short imperative command with one block of YAML that you copy from the docs. The goal here is to know which block you need and where to put it. What you will learn Rollouts, rollbacks and scaling ConfigMaps and Secrets used as environment variables and volumes Requests, limits and autoscaling Placement: node selectors, affinity, taints and tolerations Deployments, rollouts and rollbacks kubectl create deployment web --image=nginx:1.26 --replicas=3 kubectl set image deployment/web nginx=nginx:1.27 kubectl rollout status deployment/web kubectl rollout history deployment/web kubectl rollout undo deployment/web kubectl rollout undo deployment/web --to-revision=1 kubectl scale deployment/web --replicas=5 The container name for set image is the name inside the pod template, which for kubectl create deployment equals the image base name. If unsure, kubectl get deployment web -o jsonpath=&#39;{.spec.template.spec.containers[*].name}&#39; tells you. The rolling update strategy, with maxSurge and maxUnavailable , is set under spec.strategy ; know where it lives. ConfigMaps and Secrets kubectl create configmap app --from-literal=LOG_LEVEL=debug kubectl create secret generic db --from-literal=password=s3cret Use them in a pod as environment variables or files: spec: containers: - name: app image: nginx:1.27 env: - name: LOG_LEVEL valueFrom: configMapKeyRef: name: app key: LOG_LEVEL envFrom: - secretRef: name: db volumeMounts: - name: cfg mountPath: /etc/app volumes: - name: cfg configMap: name: app Secrets are base64 encoded, not encrypted; kubectl get secret db -o jsonpath=&#39;{.data.password}&#39; | base64 -d decodes one. Mounting a ConfigMap as a volume creates a file per key, and mounted files update after a while when the ConfigMap changes, while environment variables do not. Resources and quotas Requests are what the scheduler reserves; limits are the ceiling at runtime. A container that exceeds its memory limit is killed with an out-of-memory status, and one that exceeds its CPU limit is throttled. resources: requests: cpu: 100m memory: 128Mi limits: cpu: 500m memory: 256Mi A LimitRange sets default and maximum values per namespace, and a ResourceQuota caps the total. A task that says \"pods in this namespace must not exceed X\" usually means one of these. A pod that asks for more than any node can offer stays Pending, with an event that says insufficient CPU or memory. Autoscaling A HorizontalPodAutoscaler needs metrics, normally from the metrics server, and CPU requests set on the target pods. A compact definition: apiVersion: autoscaling/v2 kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler metadata: name: web spec: scaleTargetRef: apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment name: web minReplicas: 2 maxReplicas: 6 metrics: - type: Resource resource: name: cpu target: type: Utilization averageUtilization: 60 Check with kubectl get hpa . If the target shows \u003cunknown> , metrics are missing or the pods have no CPU request. Placing pods Four tools, from simplest to most flexible: nodeSelector : match a label on the node. kubectl label node w1 disk=ssd , then nodeSelector: {disk: ssd} in the pod spec. Node affinity : required or preferred rules with operators; more expressive than a selector. Taints and tolerations : a taint repels pods from a node, and a toleration lets a pod ignore it. Pod affinity and anti-affinity : place pods near or away from other pods, using a topology key such as kubernetes.io/hostname . kubectl taint nodes w1 dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule kubectl taint nodes w1 dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule- kubectl describe node w1 | grep -i taint The trailing minus removes the taint. A toleration matching it looks like: tolerations: - key: dedicated operator: Equal value: gpu effect: NoSchedule A toleration allows scheduling on a tainted node; it does not require it. To force pods onto those nodes, co"},"k8s-exam/cka-services-ingress-gateway-api-and-dns-tasks":{"html":"\u003cp>Services and networking is about a fifth of the CKA. The tasks are short, but the objects interact, so a missed label or port is easy to make. This lesson gives the shapes you need and a checking routine.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Service types and what each is for\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ingress and Gateway API routing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Network policies, including default deny\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>DNS names and CoreDNS checks\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"services\">Services\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl expose deployment web --port=80 --target-port=8080\nkubectl expose deployment web --name=web-np --port=80 --target-port=8080 --type=NodePort\nkubectl get svc web\nkubectl get endpoints web\nkubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl expose deployment web --port=80 --target-port=8080\nkubectl expose deployment web --name=web-np --port=80 --target-port=8080 --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>=NodePort\nkubectl get svc web\nkubectl get endpoints web\nkubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A ClusterIP service gives a stable internal address. NodePort opens a port on every node. LoadBalancer asks the cloud provider for an external load balancer. A headless service, with \u003ccode>clusterIP: None\u003c/code>, returns pod addresses directly in DNS. The check that matters most is the endpoints list: if it is empty, the service selector does not match any ready pod. Compare \u003ccode>kubectl get svc web -o yaml\u003c/code> selector with pod labels.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ingress\">Ingress\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An Ingress routes HTTP and HTTPS traffic to services, and it needs an Ingress controller running in the cluster. The Ingress resource alone does nothing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Ingress\nmetadata:\n  name: web\n  annotations:\n    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /\nspec:\n  ingressClassName: nginx\n  rules:\n    - host: shop.example.com\n      http:\n        paths:\n          - path: /api\n            pathType: Prefix\n            backend:\n              service:\n                name: api\n                port:\n                  number: 80\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Ingress\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">annotations:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ingressClassName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">host:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop.example.com\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">http:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">paths:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/api\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pathType:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Prefix\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">backend:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">service:\u003c/span>\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span>\n                  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">number:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">80\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Notice \u003ccode>pathType\u003c/code>, which is required, and the backend structure. You can generate a starting point with \u003ccode>kubectl create ingress web --rule=&quot;shop.example.com/api*=api:80&quot; --dry-run=client -o yaml\u003c/code>. TLS adds a \u003ccode>tls\u003c/code> section naming a secret created with \u003ccode>kubectl create secret tls\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"gateway-api\">Gateway API\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The Gateway API is the newer, more expressive model, and appears in the current curriculum. It separates roles: a GatewayClass names a controller, a Gateway defines listeners, and routes such as HTTPRoute attach to a Gateway.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: HTTPRoute\nmetadata:\n  name: web\nspec:\n  parentRefs:\n    - name: main-gateway\n  hostnames:\n    - shop.example.com\n  rules:\n    - matches:\n        - path:\n            type: PathPrefix\n            value: /api\n      backendRefs:\n        - name: api\n          port: 80\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">HTTPRoute\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">parentRefs:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">main-gateway\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hostnames:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop.example.com\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matches:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">PathPrefix\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">value:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/api\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">backendRefs:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">80\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Check status with \u003ccode>kubectl describe httproute web\u003c/code>: conditions say whether it was accepted by the gateway and whether backend references resolved. The Gateway API CRDs may need to be installed; the docs show how.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"network-policies\">Network policies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>By default all pods can talk to all pods. A NetworkPolicy selects pods and allows only listed traffic once any policy selects them. Policies need a network plugin that enforces them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: default-deny\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  podSelector: {}\n  policyTypes:\n    - Ingress\n    - Egress\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">NetworkPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default-deny\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span> {}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">policyTypes:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Ingress\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Egress\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Then allow what you need:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: allow-web-to-api\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  podSelector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: api\n  policyTypes:\n    - Ingress\n  ingress:\n    - from:\n        - podSelector:\n            matchLabels:\n              app: web\n      ports:\n        - protocol: TCP\n          port: 8080\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">NetworkPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">allow-web-to-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">policyTypes:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Ingress\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ingress:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">from:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ports:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">protocol:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">TCP\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Two traps: egress default deny also blocks DNS, so allow port 53 to the DNS pods; and a \u003ccode>from\u003c/code> with both \u003ccode>namespaceSelector\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>podSelector\u003c/code> in the same list item means &quot;both must match&quot;, while two separate items mean &quot;either&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dns-and-coredns\">DNS and CoreDNS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Services get names like \u003ccode>web.shop.svc.cluster.local\u003c/code>, and within the same namespace \u003ccode>web\u003c/code> is enough. CoreDNS runs as a deployment in \u003ccode>kube-system\u003c/code> with a ConfigMap named \u003ccode>coredns\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl run dns --rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- nslookup web.shop\nkubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns\nkubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns\nkubectl -n kube-system get configmap coredns -o yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl run dns --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- nslookup web.shop\nkubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns\nkubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns\nkubectl -n kube-system get configmap coredns -o yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If DNS fails inside pods, check that the CoreDNS pods are running, the \u003ccode>kube-dns\u003c/code> service has endpoints, and no network policy blocks port 53 traffic.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"debug-from-the-client\">Debug from the client\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When a service does not answer, test in order: does the name resolve, does the service have endpoints, does the pod answer on its own IP and port, and does a policy block the route? Use a temporary pod to curl each.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"svc_selector = {&quot;app&quot;: &quot;web&quot;}\npods = [{&quot;name&quot;: &quot;web-1&quot;, &quot;labels&quot;: {&quot;app&quot;: &quot;web&quot;}, &quot;ready&quot;: True},\n        {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;web-2&quot;, &quot;labels&quot;: {&quot;app&quot;: &quot;web&quot;}, &quot;ready&quot;: False},\n        {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;api-1&quot;, &quot;labels&quot;: {&quot;app&quot;: &quot;api&quot;}, &quot;ready&quot;: True}]\nendpoints = [p[&quot;name&quot;] for p in pods if p[&quot;ready&quot;] and all(p[&quot;labels&quot;].get(k) == v for k, v in svc_selector.items())]\nprint(&quot;endpoints:&quot;, endpoints)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">svc_selector = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web&quot;\u003c/span>}\npods = [{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web-1&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;labels&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web&quot;\u003c/span>}, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ready&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>},\n        {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web-2&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;labels&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web&quot;\u003c/span>}, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ready&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>},\n        {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;api-1&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;labels&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;api&quot;\u003c/span>}, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ready&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>}]\nendpoints = [p[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> p \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> pods \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> p[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ready&quot;\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">and\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">all\u003c/span>(p[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;labels&quot;\u003c/span>].get(k) == v \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> k, v \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> svc_selector.items())]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;endpoints:&quot;\u003c/span>, endpoints)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"4\" data-edges=\"cli&gt;dns;cli&gt;svc;ing&gt;svc;pol&gt;pod;svc&gt;pod\" style=\"--rows:4\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Reaching an application\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"12.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"62.5\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"87.5\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cli\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:37.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Client pod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dns\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:12.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CoreDNS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"svc\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:37.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pol\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:87.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Network policy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ing\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:62.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Ingress or route\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pod\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:37.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Ready pods\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Client pod to CoreDNS; 2, Client pod to Service; 3, Ingress or route to Service; 4, Network policy to Ready pods; 5, Service to Ready pods.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cli\">\u003cdt>Client pod \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Resolves a name and connects.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> DNS egress blocked by policy.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dns\">\u003cdt>CoreDNS \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Resolves service names.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> CoreDNS pods not running.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"svc\">\u003cdt>Service \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stable address and selector.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Selector matches no pods.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pol\">\u003cdt>Network policy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Allows or blocks the connection.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Default deny without an allow.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ing\">\u003cdt>Ingress or route \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> External HTTP entry.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No controller installed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pod\">\u003cdt>Ready pods \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Endpoints behind the service.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Failing readiness probes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-dns-blocked-by-default-deny\">Incident: DNS blocked by default deny\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After adding a default deny egress policy, applications could not reach anything by name.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl exec web-1 -n shop -- nslookup api\nkubectl get networkpolicy -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> web-1 -n shop -- nslookup api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">;; connection timed out; no servers could be reached\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get networkpolicy -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME           POD-SELECTOR   AGE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">default-deny   &lt;none&gt;         3m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The deny policy applied to all pods in the namespace, including their DNS lookups to CoreDNS. Adding an egress rule allowing UDP and TCP port 53 to pods in \u003ccode>kube-system\u003c/code> restored name resolution. Whenever you apply egress deny, add DNS in the same change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Creating an Ingress with no controller installed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Omitting pathType in an Ingress\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A service selector that does not match any pod label\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Blocking DNS with an egress default deny\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Misreading namespaceSelector and podSelector in one entry as &quot;or&quot;\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"services","text":"Services"},{"id":"ingress","text":"Ingress"},{"id":"gateway-api","text":"Gateway API"},{"id":"network-policies","text":"Network policies"},{"id":"dns-and-coredns","text":"DNS and CoreDNS"},{"id":"debug-from-the-client","text":"Debug from the client"},{"id":"incident-dns-blocked-by-default-deny","text":"Incident: DNS blocked by default deny"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: services","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/"},{"t":"Gateway API","u":"https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: network policies","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/"}],"text":"Services and networking is about a fifth of the CKA. The tasks are short, but the objects interact, so a missed label or port is easy to make. This lesson gives the shapes you need and a checking routine. What you will learn Service types and what each is for Ingress and Gateway API routing Network policies, including default deny DNS names and CoreDNS checks Services kubectl expose deployment web --port=80 --target-port=8080 kubectl expose deployment web --name=web-np --port=80 --target-port=8080 -- type =NodePort kubectl get svc web kubectl get endpoints web kubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web A ClusterIP service gives a stable internal address. NodePort opens a port on every node. LoadBalancer asks the cloud provider for an external load balancer. A headless service, with clusterIP: None , returns pod addresses directly in DNS. The check that matters most is the endpoints list: if it is empty, the service selector does not match any ready pod. Compare kubectl get svc web -o yaml selector with pod labels. Ingress An Ingress routes HTTP and HTTPS traffic to services, and it needs an Ingress controller running in the cluster. The Ingress resource alone does nothing. apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: Ingress metadata: name: web annotations: nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: / spec: ingressClassName: nginx rules: - host: shop.example.com http: paths: - path: /api pathType: Prefix backend: service: name: api port: number: 80 Notice pathType , which is required, and the backend structure. You can generate a starting point with kubectl create ingress web --rule=\"shop.example.com/api*=api:80\" --dry-run=client -o yaml . TLS adds a tls section naming a secret created with kubectl create secret tls . Gateway API The Gateway API is the newer, more expressive model, and appears in the current curriculum. It separates roles: a GatewayClass names a controller, a Gateway defines listeners, and routes such as HTTPRoute attach to a Gateway. apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: HTTPRoute metadata: name: web spec: parentRefs: - name: main-gateway hostnames: - shop.example.com rules: - matches: - path: type: PathPrefix value: /api backendRefs: - name: api port: 80 Check status with kubectl describe httproute web : conditions say whether it was accepted by the gateway and whether backend references resolved. The Gateway API CRDs may need to be installed; the docs show how. Network policies By default all pods can talk to all pods. A NetworkPolicy selects pods and allows only listed traffic once any policy selects them. Policies need a network plugin that enforces them. apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: default-deny namespace: shop spec: podSelector: {} policyTypes: - Ingress - Egress Then allow what you need: apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: allow-web-to-api namespace: shop spec: podSelector: matchLabels: app: api policyTypes: - Ingress ingress: - from: - podSelector: matchLabels: app: web ports: - protocol: TCP port: 8080 Two traps: egress default deny also blocks DNS, so allow port 53 to the DNS pods; and a from with both namespaceSelector and podSelector in the same list item means \"both must match\", while two separate items mean \"either\". DNS and CoreDNS Services get names like web.shop.svc.cluster.local , and within the same namespace web is enough. CoreDNS runs as a deployment in kube-system with a ConfigMap named coredns . kubectl run dns -- rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- nslookup web.shop kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns kubectl -n kube-system get configmap coredns -o yaml If DNS fails inside pods, check that the CoreDNS pods are running, the kube-dns service has endpoints, and no network policy blocks port 53 traffic. Debug from the client When a service does not answer, test in order: does the name resolve, does the service have endpoin"},"k8s-exam/cka-storage-tasks":{"html":"\u003cp>Storage is the smallest CKA domain by weight, about a tenth, yet the tasks are specific and a single wrong field stops a claim from binding. The Kubernetes module explains the model. Here you practise the objects and the failure patterns.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>PersistentVolumes, claims and how binding works\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>StorageClasses and dynamic provisioning\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Access modes, volume modes and reclaim policies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mounting, expanding and debugging claims\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-objects\">The objects\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>PersistentVolume\u003c/strong> (PV) is a piece of storage in the cluster. A \u003cstrong>PersistentVolumeClaim\u003c/strong> (PVC) is a request for storage by a user. A pod mounts a claim, not the volume directly. A \u003cstrong>StorageClass\u003c/strong> describes a kind of storage and how to provision it dynamically.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A static volume, backed by a directory on a node, as used in exercises:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: v1\nkind: PersistentVolume\nmetadata:\n  name: pv-data\nspec:\n  capacity:\n    storage: 5Gi\n  accessModes:\n    - ReadWriteOnce\n  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain\n  storageClassName: manual\n  hostPath:\n    path: /mnt/data\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">PersistentVolume\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">pv-data\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">capacity:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">storage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">5Gi\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">accessModes:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ReadWriteOnce\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Retain\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">storageClassName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">manual\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hostPath:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/mnt/data\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A claim that can bind to it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: v1\nkind: PersistentVolumeClaim\nmetadata:\n  name: data\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  accessModes:\n    - ReadWriteOnce\n  resources:\n    requests:\n      storage: 2Gi\n  storageClassName: manual\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">PersistentVolumeClaim\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">data\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">accessModes:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ReadWriteOnce\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">requests:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">storage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">2Gi\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">storageClassName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">manual\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Binding requires the claim&#39;s class, access modes and capacity request to be satisfied by a volume: the volume must be at least as large, include the requested access mode and match the storage class name. A claim without a storage class name uses the cluster&#39;s default class if one exists; set \u003ccode>storageClassName: &quot;&quot;\u003c/code> to request a volume with no class.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"use-the-claim-in-a-pod\">Use the claim in a pod\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: app\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  containers:\n    - name: app\n      image: nginx:1.27\n      volumeMounts:\n        - name: data\n          mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html\n  volumes:\n    - name: data\n      persistentVolumeClaim:\n        claimName: data\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Pod\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">app\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">app\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx:1.27\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumeMounts:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">data\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mountPath:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/usr/share/nginx/html\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumes:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">data\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">persistentVolumeClaim:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">claimName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">data\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The claim must be in the same namespace as the pod. Volumes themselves are cluster-scoped.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"access-modes\">Access modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>ReadWriteOnce\u003c/code> (RWO): mounted read-write by one node\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>ReadOnlyMany\u003c/code> (ROX): mounted read-only by many nodes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>ReadWriteMany\u003c/code> (RWX): mounted read-write by many nodes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>ReadWriteOncePod\u003c/code> (RWOP): mounted read-write by one pod only\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Not every storage type supports every mode, so a request for RWX on a block disk will not bind. RWO is per node, so several pods on the same node can share it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"storageclasses-and-dynamic-provisioning\">StorageClasses and dynamic provisioning\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1\nkind: StorageClass\nmetadata:\n  name: fast\nprovisioner: example.com/provisioner\nreclaimPolicy: Delete\nallowVolumeExpansion: true\nvolumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">storage.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">StorageClass\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">fast\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">provisioner:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">example.com/provisioner\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">reclaimPolicy:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Delete\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">allowVolumeExpansion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumeBindingMode:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">WaitForFirstConsumer\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With dynamic provisioning, creating a claim that names the class makes the provisioner create a volume. \u003ccode>WaitForFirstConsumer\u003c/code> delays binding until a pod is scheduled, so the volume is created where the pod can use it; that is why a claim can stay \u003ccode>Pending\u003c/code> until a pod uses it, and that is not an error. A default class is marked with the annotation \u003ccode>storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class: &quot;true&quot;\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reclaim-policy\">Reclaim policy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>What happens to the volume when its claim is deleted:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>Retain\u003c/code>: the volume stays with its data and must be cleaned manually; it shows \u003ccode>Released\u003c/code> and will not bind again until the old claim reference is cleared\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>Delete\u003c/code>: the volume and the backing storage are deleted\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>Recycle\u003c/code> is deprecated\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>You can change the policy on an existing PV with \u003ccode>kubectl patch pv pv-data -p &#39;{&quot;spec&quot;:{&quot;persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy&quot;:&quot;Retain&quot;}}&#39;\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"expanding-a-claim\">Expanding a claim\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If the class allows expansion, edit the claim and increase \u003ccode>spec.resources.requests.storage\u003c/code>. The size can only grow. Some drivers need the pod to restart before the filesystem resize completes, and the claim shows conditions describing progress.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"other-volume-types\">Other volume types\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Know the short list: \u003ccode>emptyDir\u003c/code> (scratch space that lives as long as the pod), \u003ccode>hostPath\u003c/code> (a path on the node, risky), \u003ccode>configMap\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>secret\u003c/code> (configuration), \u003ccode>projected\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>persistentVolumeClaim\u003c/code>. Tasks may ask for two containers sharing an \u003ccode>emptyDir\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"debug-a-claim\">Debug a claim\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pv,pvc -A\nkubectl describe pvc data -n shop\nkubectl get storageclass\nkubectl describe pod app -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get pv,pvc -A\nkubectl describe pvc data -n shop\nkubectl get storageclass\nkubectl describe pod app -n shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The events on the claim and pod show what is missing: no matching volume, no class, a provisioner that is not running or a node that cannot mount.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pvs = [{&quot;name&quot;: &quot;pv-data&quot;, &quot;size&quot;: 5, &quot;modes&quot;: {&quot;RWO&quot;}, &quot;cls&quot;: &quot;manual&quot;, &quot;free&quot;: True}]\nclaim = {&quot;size&quot;: 2, &quot;modes&quot;: {&quot;RWO&quot;}, &quot;cls&quot;: &quot;manual&quot;}\nmatch = [p[&quot;name&quot;] for p in pvs if p[&quot;free&quot;] and p[&quot;size&quot;] &gt;= claim[&quot;size&quot;] and claim[&quot;modes&quot;] &lt;= p[&quot;modes&quot;] and p[&quot;cls&quot;] == claim[&quot;cls&quot;]]\nprint(&quot;binds to:&quot;, match)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">pvs = [{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pv-data&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;size&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;modes&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;RWO&quot;\u003c/span>}, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cls&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;manual&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;free&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>}]\nclaim = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;size&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;modes&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;RWO&quot;\u003c/span>}, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cls&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;manual&quot;\u003c/span>}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">match\u003c/span> = [p[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> p \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> pvs \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> p[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;free&quot;\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">and\u003c/span> p[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;size&quot;\u003c/span>] &gt;= claim[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;size&quot;\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">and\u003c/span> claim[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;modes&quot;\u003c/span>] &lt;= p[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;modes&quot;\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">and\u003c/span> p[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cls&quot;\u003c/span>] == claim[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cls&quot;\u003c/span>]]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;binds to:&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">match\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"4\" data-edges=\"sc&gt;pvc;pvc&gt;pv;pv&gt;pod;pod&gt;ctr;pv&gt;rec\" style=\"--rows:4\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">From claim to mounted storage\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"12.5\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"87.5\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sc\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:12.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">StorageClass\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pvc\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">PersistentVolumeClaim\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pv\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">PersistentVolume\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pod\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ctr\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Container path\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rec\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:87.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Reclaim policy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, StorageClass to PersistentVolumeClaim; 2, PersistentVolumeClaim to PersistentVolume; 3, PersistentVolume to Pod; 4, Pod to Container path; 5, PersistentVolume to Reclaim policy.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sc\">\u003cdt>StorageClass \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Type of storage and provisioner.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No default class set.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pvc\">\u003cdt>PersistentVolumeClaim \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Size, mode and class requested.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Class name typo.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pv\">\u003cdt>PersistentVolume \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Static or dynamically created.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too small or wrong mode.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pod\">\u003cdt>Pod \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Mounts the claim by name.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Claim in another namespace.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ctr\">\u003cdt>Container path \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Data visible at the mount path.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong mountPath.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rec\">\u003cdt>Reclaim policy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Retain or delete on claim removal.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Data lost on Delete.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-claim-that-stayed-pending\">Incident: The claim that stayed Pending\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A claim stayed Pending, and the candidate suspected the provisioner. A pod using it had never been created.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pvc data -n shop\nkubectl describe pvc data -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pvc data -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME   STATUS    VOLUME   CAPACITY   ACCESS MODES   STORAGECLASS   AGE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">data   Pending                                      fast           6m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pvc data -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Events:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  Normal  WaitForFirstConsumer  6m  persistentvolume-controller  waiting for first consumer to be created before binding\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The storage class used \u003ccode>WaitForFirstConsumer\u003c/code>, so nothing bound until a pod referenced the claim. The event said so plainly. Creating the pod made the volume appear and the claim bind. Reading the events before changing anything saved time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Requesting a size or mode no volume can satisfy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A storage class name mismatch between PV and PVC\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating the claim in a different namespace from the pod\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting a Pending claim to bind before any pod uses it, with WaitForFirstConsumer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that Retain leaves volumes Released and not reusable until cleaned\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-objects","text":"The objects"},{"id":"use-the-claim-in-a-pod","text":"Use the claim in a pod"},{"id":"access-modes","text":"Access modes"},{"id":"storageclasses-and-dynamic-provisioning","text":"StorageClasses and dynamic provisioning"},{"id":"reclaim-policy","text":"Reclaim policy"},{"id":"expanding-a-claim","text":"Expanding a claim"},{"id":"other-volume-types","text":"Other volume types"},{"id":"debug-a-claim","text":"Debug a claim"},{"id":"incident-the-claim-that-stayed-pending","text":"Incident: The claim that stayed Pending"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: persistent volumes","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: storage classes","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/storage-classes/"}],"text":"Storage is the smallest CKA domain by weight, about a tenth, yet the tasks are specific and a single wrong field stops a claim from binding. The Kubernetes module explains the model. Here you practise the objects and the failure patterns. What you will learn PersistentVolumes, claims and how binding works StorageClasses and dynamic provisioning Access modes, volume modes and reclaim policies Mounting, expanding and debugging claims The objects A PersistentVolume (PV) is a piece of storage in the cluster. A PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) is a request for storage by a user. A pod mounts a claim, not the volume directly. A StorageClass describes a kind of storage and how to provision it dynamically. A static volume, backed by a directory on a node, as used in exercises: apiVersion: v1 kind: PersistentVolume metadata: name: pv-data spec: capacity: storage: 5Gi accessModes: - ReadWriteOnce persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain storageClassName: manual hostPath: path: /mnt/data A claim that can bind to it: apiVersion: v1 kind: PersistentVolumeClaim metadata: name: data namespace: shop spec: accessModes: - ReadWriteOnce resources: requests: storage: 2Gi storageClassName: manual Binding requires the claim&#39;s class, access modes and capacity request to be satisfied by a volume: the volume must be at least as large, include the requested access mode and match the storage class name. A claim without a storage class name uses the cluster&#39;s default class if one exists; set storageClassName: \"\" to request a volume with no class. Use the claim in a pod apiVersion: v1 kind: Pod metadata: name: app namespace: shop spec: containers: - name: app image: nginx:1.27 volumeMounts: - name: data mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html volumes: - name: data persistentVolumeClaim: claimName: data The claim must be in the same namespace as the pod. Volumes themselves are cluster-scoped. Access modes ReadWriteOnce (RWO): mounted read-write by one node ReadOnlyMany (ROX): mounted read-only by many nodes ReadWriteMany (RWX): mounted read-write by many nodes ReadWriteOncePod (RWOP): mounted read-write by one pod only Not every storage type supports every mode, so a request for RWX on a block disk will not bind. RWO is per node, so several pods on the same node can share it. StorageClasses and dynamic provisioning apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1 kind: StorageClass metadata: name: fast provisioner: example.com/provisioner reclaimPolicy: Delete allowVolumeExpansion: true volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer With dynamic provisioning, creating a claim that names the class makes the provisioner create a volume. WaitForFirstConsumer delays binding until a pod is scheduled, so the volume is created where the pod can use it; that is why a claim can stay Pending until a pod uses it, and that is not an error. A default class is marked with the annotation storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class: \"true\" . Reclaim policy What happens to the volume when its claim is deleted: Retain : the volume stays with its data and must be cleaned manually; it shows Released and will not bind again until the old claim reference is cleared Delete : the volume and the backing storage are deleted Recycle is deprecated You can change the policy on an existing PV with kubectl patch pv pv-data -p &#39;{\"spec\":{\"persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy\":\"Retain\"}}&#39; . Expanding a claim If the class allows expansion, edit the claim and increase spec.resources.requests.storage . The size can only grow. Some drivers need the pod to restart before the filesystem resize completes, and the claim shows conditions describing progress. Other volume types Know the short list: emptyDir (scratch space that lives as long as the pod), hostPath (a path on the node, risky), configMap and secret (configuration), projected , and persistentVolumeClaim . Tasks may ask for two containers sharing an emptyDir . Debug a claim kubectl get pv,pvc -A kubectl describe pvc data -n shop kubectl get storageclass kubectl describe"},"k8s-exam/cka-troubleshooting-method-and-scenarios":{"html":"\u003cp>Troubleshooting carries about thirty percent of the CKA, and in real life it is the skill that matters most. The exam gives you a broken thing and a time limit. The people who do well are not the ones who know the most obscure flag; they are the ones who look in the right order and read what the system tells them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A repeatable top-to-bottom order of checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Application and service failures\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Node failures: kubelet, runtime and network\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Control plane failures and certificates\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-method\">The method\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Define the symptom.\u003c/strong> What exactly fails, where, and since when?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Look at the object.\u003c/strong> \u003ccode>kubectl get\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>kubectl describe\u003c/code>, read the events.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Look at the logs.\u003c/strong> Container logs, previous logs, component logs.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Go down a layer.\u003c/strong> Pod, then node, then control plane, then infrastructure.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Change one thing at a time\u003c/strong> and verify.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Avoid guessing. Each command should answer a question you can state.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pod-failures\">Pod failures\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -A\nkubectl describe pod web-1 -n shop\nkubectl logs web-1 -n shop\nkubectl logs web-1 -n shop --previous\nkubectl get events -n shop --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get pods -A\nkubectl describe pod web-1 -n shop\nkubectl logs web-1 -n shop\nkubectl logs web-1 -n shop --previous\nkubectl get events -n shop --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Map the status to the cause:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Status\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Usual cause\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Where to look\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Pending\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No node fits, unbound claim\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>describe events\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>ImagePullBackOff\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Wrong image name or tag, registry access\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>describe events\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>CrashLoopBackOff\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Process exits, bad config or command\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>logs --previous\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>CreateContainerConfigError\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Missing ConfigMap or Secret key\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>describe events\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>OOMKilled in last state\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Memory limit too low\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>describe, limits\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Running but not Ready\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Failing readiness probe\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>describe, probe settings\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"service-failures\">Service failures\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Work from the client to the pod: does the name resolve, does the service have endpoints, does the pod respond on the target port, and does a policy block it? The most common causes are a selector that does not match pod labels, a wrong \u003ccode>targetPort\u003c/code>, readiness probes that fail so pods are not in the endpoints, and network policies.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"node-failures\">Node failures\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When \u003ccode>kubectl get nodes\u003c/code> shows \u003ccode>NotReady\u003c/code>, log in to the node and check the kubelet and the runtime:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"systemctl status kubelet\njournalctl -u kubelet -n 50 --no-pager\nsystemctl status containerd\ncrictl ps -a\ncat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">systemctl status kubelet\njournalctl -u kubelet -n 50 --no-pager\nsystemctl status containerd\ncrictl ps -a\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Typical findings: the kubelet service is stopped or disabled (start and enable it), a wrong path in its configuration or service drop-in, an expired or wrong certificate, a runtime socket that does not exist, swap enabled, or a full disk. After a fix, \u003ccode>systemctl daemon-reload\u003c/code> if you changed a unit file, then \u003ccode>systemctl restart kubelet\u003c/code>, and check again from the control plane.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"control-plane-failures\">Control plane failures\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>On a kubeadm cluster the control plane components are static pods. If \u003ccode>kubectl\u003c/code> cannot connect at all, the API server is the first suspect. Look from the node:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"crictl ps -a | grep -E &quot;kube-apiserver|etcd|scheduler|controller&quot;\ncrictl logs CONTAINER_ID\nls /etc/kubernetes/manifests\njournalctl -u kubelet -n 50 --no-pager\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">crictl ps -a | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;kube-apiserver|etcd|scheduler|controller&quot;\u003c/span>\ncrictl logs CONTAINER_ID\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> /etc/kubernetes/manifests\njournalctl -u kubelet -n 50 --no-pager\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Typical faults: a typo or wrong path in a manifest, a wrong certificate file name, a wrong etcd address, a port clash. The kubelet log often says that it cannot create the pod and why. When only a component such as the scheduler is down, pods stay Pending with no events from the scheduler, and \u003ccode>kubectl get pods -n kube-system\u003c/code> shows its pod failing. Fix the manifest and the kubelet recreates the pod.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If workloads start but never become Ready or deployments do not roll, look at the controller manager. If pods are created but never scheduled, look at the scheduler. Know the division.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"certificates\">Certificates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubeadm certs check-expiration\nkubeadm certs renew all\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubeadm certs check-expiration\nkubeadm certs renew all\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Expired certificates cause confusing errors such as failing \u003ccode>kubectl\u003c/code> or kubelet connections. After renewal, restart the control plane static pods by moving their manifests away and back, or restart the kubelet, as the docs describe.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"networking-and-dns\">Networking and DNS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pods that cannot resolve names point to CoreDNS or a policy. Nodes that cannot reach each other point to the network plugin pods in \u003ccode>kube-system\u003c/code>. A plugin that is not running makes new pods stay in \u003ccode>ContainerCreating\u003c/code> with network errors in the events.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"resource-and-capacity-problems\">Resource and capacity problems\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>kubectl top nodes\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>kubectl top pods\u003c/code> need the metrics server. A node with \u003ccode>MemoryPressure\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>DiskPressure\u003c/code> conditions evicts pods; check \u003ccode>kubectl describe node\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"write-down-what-you-find\">Write down what you find\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When a task asks you to record the cause, such as &quot;write the reason the pod failed to a file&quot;, copy exact text from the events or logs rather than paraphrasing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"symptoms = {\n    &quot;ImagePullBackOff&quot;: &quot;check image name, tag and registry access&quot;,\n    &quot;CrashLoopBackOff&quot;: &quot;kubectl logs --previous&quot;,\n    &quot;Pending&quot;: &quot;kubectl describe pod, read scheduler events&quot;,\n    &quot;NotReady node&quot;: &quot;systemctl status kubelet and journalctl&quot;,\n}\nfor s, hint in symptoms.items():\n    print(f&quot;{s:18} {hint}&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">symptoms = {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ImagePullBackOff&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;check image name, tag and registry access&quot;\u003c/span>,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;CrashLoopBackOff&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;kubectl logs --previous&quot;\u003c/span>,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Pending&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;kubectl describe pod, read scheduler events&quot;\u003c/span>,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;NotReady node&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;systemctl status kubelet and journalctl&quot;\u003c/span>,\n}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> s, hint \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> symptoms.items():\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{s:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">18\u003c/span>}\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{hint}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"sym&gt;obj;obj&gt;log;log&gt;node;log&gt;cp;node&gt;fix;cp&gt;fix\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A top-to-bottom troubleshooting order\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sym\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Symptom\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"obj\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Object and events\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"log\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Logs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"node\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:16.7%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Node and kubelet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cp\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:83.3%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Control plane\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fix\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Fix and verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Symptom to Object and events; 2, Object and events to Logs; 3, Logs to Node and kubelet; 4, Logs to Control plane; 5, Node and kubelet to Fix and verify; 6, Control plane to Fix and verify.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sym\">\u003cdt>Symptom \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> State what fails and where.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Fixing before understanding.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"obj\">\u003cdt>Object and events \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> get, describe and events.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not reading the events.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"log\">\u003cdt>Logs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Container and previous logs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing --previous.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"node\">\u003cdt>Node and kubelet \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> systemctl, journalctl and crictl.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Kubelet disabled after reboot.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cp\">\u003cdt>Control plane \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Static pod manifests and certificates.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Typo in a manifest path.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fix\">\u003cdt>Fix and verify \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> One change, then check.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Changing many things at once.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-kubelet-that-was-stopped-not-broken\">Incident: The kubelet that was stopped, not broken\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A node was NotReady. The candidate spent ten minutes on pod networking before looking at the node itself.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"systemctl status kubelet\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemctl status kubelet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">kubelet.service - kubelet: The Kubernetes Node Agent\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">     Loaded: loaded (/lib/systemd/system/kubelet.service; disabled)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">     Active: inactive (dead)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The kubelet was inactive and disabled, so it had not started after a reboot. Starting and enabling it, then checking \u003ccode>kubectl get nodes\u003c/code>, fixed the task in under a minute. The lesson is to check the lowest layer a symptom could involve before digging into higher layers that depend on it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Looking at pods when the node is down\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting logs --previous for a crashed container\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing several things before testing the first\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not checking the kubelet log when a static pod does not start\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Paraphrasing an error when the task asks for the exact reason\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-method","text":"The method"},{"id":"pod-failures","text":"Pod failures"},{"id":"service-failures","text":"Service failures"},{"id":"node-failures","text":"Node failures"},{"id":"control-plane-failures","text":"Control plane failures"},{"id":"certificates","text":"Certificates"},{"id":"networking-and-dns","text":"Networking and DNS"},{"id":"resource-and-capacity-problems","text":"Resource and capacity problems"},{"id":"write-down-what-you-find","text":"Write down what you find"},{"id":"incident-the-kubelet-that-was-stopped-not-broken","text":"Incident: The kubelet that was stopped, not broken"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: troubleshooting clusters","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-cluster/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: debug pods","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-application/debug-pods/"}],"text":"Troubleshooting carries about thirty percent of the CKA, and in real life it is the skill that matters most. The exam gives you a broken thing and a time limit. The people who do well are not the ones who know the most obscure flag; they are the ones who look in the right order and read what the system tells them. What you will learn A repeatable top-to-bottom order of checks Application and service failures Node failures: kubelet, runtime and network Control plane failures and certificates The method Define the symptom. What exactly fails, where, and since when? Look at the object. kubectl get and kubectl describe , read the events. Look at the logs. Container logs, previous logs, component logs. Go down a layer. Pod, then node, then control plane, then infrastructure. Change one thing at a time and verify. Avoid guessing. Each command should answer a question you can state. Pod failures kubectl get pods -A kubectl describe pod web-1 -n shop kubectl logs web-1 -n shop kubectl logs web-1 -n shop --previous kubectl get events -n shop --sort-by=.lastTimestamp Map the status to the cause: Status Usual cause Where to look Pending No node fits, unbound claim describe events ImagePullBackOff Wrong image name or tag, registry access describe events CrashLoopBackOff Process exits, bad config or command logs --previous CreateContainerConfigError Missing ConfigMap or Secret key describe events OOMKilled in last state Memory limit too low describe, limits Running but not Ready Failing readiness probe describe, probe settings Service failures Work from the client to the pod: does the name resolve, does the service have endpoints, does the pod respond on the target port, and does a policy block it? The most common causes are a selector that does not match pod labels, a wrong targetPort , readiness probes that fail so pods are not in the endpoints, and network policies. Node failures When kubectl get nodes shows NotReady , log in to the node and check the kubelet and the runtime: systemctl status kubelet journalctl -u kubelet -n 50 --no-pager systemctl status containerd crictl ps -a cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml Typical findings: the kubelet service is stopped or disabled (start and enable it), a wrong path in its configuration or service drop-in, an expired or wrong certificate, a runtime socket that does not exist, swap enabled, or a full disk. After a fix, systemctl daemon-reload if you changed a unit file, then systemctl restart kubelet , and check again from the control plane. Control plane failures On a kubeadm cluster the control plane components are static pods. If kubectl cannot connect at all, the API server is the first suspect. Look from the node: crictl ps -a | grep -E \"kube-apiserver|etcd|scheduler|controller\" crictl logs CONTAINER_ID ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests journalctl -u kubelet -n 50 --no-pager Typical faults: a typo or wrong path in a manifest, a wrong certificate file name, a wrong etcd address, a port clash. The kubelet log often says that it cannot create the pod and why. When only a component such as the scheduler is down, pods stay Pending with no events from the scheduler, and kubectl get pods -n kube-system shows its pod failing. Fix the manifest and the kubelet recreates the pod. If workloads start but never become Ready or deployments do not roll, look at the controller manager. If pods are created but never scheduled, look at the scheduler. Know the division. Certificates kubeadm certs check-expiration kubeadm certs renew all Expired certificates cause confusing errors such as failing kubectl or kubelet connections. After renewal, restart the control plane static pods by moving their manifests away and back, or restart the kubelet, as the docs describe. Networking and DNS Pods that cannot resolve names point to CoreDNS or a policy. Nodes that cannot reach each other point to the network plugin pods in kube-system . A plugin that is not running makes new pods stay in ContainerCreating with network errors in"},"k8s-exam/cks-cluster-setup-and-hardening":{"html":"\u003cp>The Certified Kubernetes Security Specialist exam assumes you can already administer a cluster, which is why it requires a current CKA. Cluster setup and cluster hardening together make about a quarter of it. The idea is to take a working cluster and remove the ways it can be abused. The DevSecOps module covers the principles; here are the tasks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Default deny network policy and protecting node metadata\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running and reading CIS benchmark results\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hardening API server and kubelet settings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>RBAC, service accounts and verifying binaries\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"default-deny-and-metadata\">Default deny and metadata\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start with a default deny policy in each namespace, then allow only what is needed. A deny-all for ingress and egress was shown in the networking lesson. Cloud nodes often expose a metadata service at a link-local address, which holds credentials for the node&#39;s cloud role. Pods should usually not reach it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: deny-metadata\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  podSelector: {}\n  policyTypes:\n    - Egress\n  egress:\n    - to:\n        - ipBlock:\n            cidr: 0.0.0.0/0\n            except:\n              - 169.254.169.254/32\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">NetworkPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deny-metadata\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span> {}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">policyTypes:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Egress\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">egress:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">to:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ipBlock:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cidr:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/0\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">except:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">169.254\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.169\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.254\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/32\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This allows all egress except to the metadata address. Combine it with other policies as the task requires, remembering that policies add up.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cis-benchmark-with-kube-bench\">CIS benchmark with kube-bench\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The CIS Kubernetes Benchmark is a long list of recommended settings, and kube-bench is a tool that checks a node against it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kube-bench run --targets master\nkube-bench run --targets node\nkube-bench run --targets master --check 1.2.1\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kube-bench run --targets master\nkube-bench run --targets node\nkube-bench run --targets master --check 1.2.1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The output lists each check as PASS, FAIL or WARN, with remediation text. A task usually names a failing check and asks you to fix it. The fix is almost always a flag in a static pod manifest or a kubelet configuration file. Re-run the check afterwards to confirm.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"harden-the-api-server\">Harden the API server\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Edit \u003ccode>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml\u003c/code>; the kubelet restarts the pod when you save. Useful settings:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>--anonymous-auth=false\u003c/code> rejects unauthenticated requests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>--authorization-mode=Node,RBAC\u003c/code> enables node and role-based authorization, not \u003ccode>AlwaysAllow\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>--enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction\u003c/code> limits what kubelets can change\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>--profiling=false\u003c/code> disables profiling endpoints\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>--audit-log-path\u003c/code> and related flags enable auditing (a later lesson)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Avoid insecure ports and unneeded features\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>After a change, wait for the API server to return. If it does not, read \u003ccode>crictl logs\u003c/code> for the container; the cause is usually a typo. Keep a copy of the original manifest outside the manifests directory before editing, so that you can restore it quickly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"harden-the-kubelet\">Harden the kubelet\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The kubelet configuration is a file, often \u003ccode>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml\u003c/code>. Important settings:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"authentication:\n  anonymous:\n    enabled: false\n  webhook:\n    enabled: true\nauthorization:\n  mode: Webhook\nreadOnlyPort: 0\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">authentication:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">anonymous:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">enabled:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">webhook:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">enabled:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">authorization:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mode:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Webhook\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">readOnlyPort:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Anonymous access disabled, authorization delegated to the API server and the read-only port closed. Apply with \u003ccode>systemctl restart kubelet\u003c/code>. Etcd and the control plane should also use client certificate authentication, which kubeadm sets up by default.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rbac-and-service-accounts\">RBAC and service accounts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Least privilege applies to humans and to workloads.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Avoid \u003ccode>cluster-admin\u003c/code> bindings and wildcard verbs or resources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Look for risky bindings with \u003ccode>kubectl get clusterrolebinding -o wide\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Remove permissions that are not needed, and bind roles to groups instead of many individuals\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Set \u003ccode>automountServiceAccountToken: false\u003c/code> on pods or service accounts that do not call the API\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use dedicated service accounts per workload, not the \u003ccode>default\u003c/code> account\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl patch serviceaccount default -n shop -p '{&quot;automountServiceAccountToken&quot;: false}'\nkubectl auth can-i --list --as system:serviceaccount:shop:default -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl patch serviceaccount default -n shop -p \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;automountServiceAccountToken&quot;: false}&#x27;\u003c/span>\nkubectl auth can-i --list --as system:serviceaccount:shop:default -n shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protect-ingress-and-secrets-in-transit\">Protect ingress and secrets in transit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Expose applications over TLS. Create a secret from a certificate and key, and reference it in the Ingress \u003ccode>tls\u003c/code> section:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl create secret tls web-tls --cert=tls.crt --key=tls.key -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl create secret tls web-tls --cert=tls.crt --key=tls.key -n shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"verify-the-binaries\">Verify the binaries\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A tampered binary defeats every other control. Compare the checksum of a downloaded Kubernetes binary with the published value:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sha512sum kubelet\necho &quot;&lt;expected-sha512&gt;  kubelet&quot; | sha512sum --check\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sha512sum\u003c/span> kubelet\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&lt;expected-sha512&gt;  kubelet&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sha512sum\u003c/span> --check\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A task may give you a checksum file and ask you to confirm which binary matches, or to remove the one that does not.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-up-with-versions\">Keep up with versions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Running an old version leaves known vulnerabilities in place. Plan upgrades to supported versions, as practised in the earlier CKA lesson, and subscribe to security announcements.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"checks = {\n    &quot;anonymous-auth=false&quot;: True,\n    &quot;authorization-mode includes RBAC&quot;: True,\n    &quot;NodeRestriction enabled&quot;: False,\n    &quot;kubelet read-only port closed&quot;: True,\n}\nfor name, passed in checks.items():\n    print((&quot;PASS  &quot; if passed else &quot;FAIL  &quot;) + name)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">checks = {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;anonymous-auth=false&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;authorization-mode includes RBAC&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;NodeRestriction enabled&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;kubelet read-only port closed&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>,\n}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> name, passed \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> checks.items():\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>((\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;PASS  &quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> passed \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;FAIL  &quot;\u003c/span>) + name)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"bench&gt;api;bench&gt;kub;api&gt;rb;kub&gt;rb;rb&gt;net;net&gt;ver\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Hardening a cluster\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"bench\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Benchmark\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:16.7%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">API server flags\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"kub\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:83.3%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Kubelet config\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rb\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">RBAC and accounts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"net\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Network policy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ver\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Verify and re-test\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Benchmark to API server flags; 2, Benchmark to Kubelet config; 3, API server flags to RBAC and accounts; 4, Kubelet config to RBAC and accounts; 5, RBAC and accounts to Network policy; 6, Network policy to Verify and re-test.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"bench\">\u003cdt>Benchmark \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> kube-bench against the CIS checks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Treating warnings as passes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>API server flags \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Anonymous off, RBAC on, NodeRestriction.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A typo takes the API down.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"kub\">\u003cdt>Kubelet config \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Webhook auth, no read-only port.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Config not reloaded.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rb\">\u003cdt>RBAC and accounts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Least privilege and no default token.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wildcard cluster-admin.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"net\">\u003cdt>Network policy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Default deny, block metadata.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Policies on a plugin that ignores them.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ver\">\u003cdt>Verify and re-test \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Checksums and benchmark re-run.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Trusting a binary unchecked.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-anonymous-access-left-on\">Incident: Anonymous access left on\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A benchmark flagged that the kubelet accepted anonymous requests, and a quick probe from another node listed pods without credentials.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kube-bench run --targets node --check 4.2.1\ncurl -sk https://10.0.0.12:10250/pods | head -c 80\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kube-bench run --targets node --check 4.2.1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[FAIL] 4.2.1 Ensure that the --anonymous-auth argument is set to false\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -sk https://10.0.0.12:10250/pods | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -c 80\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;kind&quot;:&quot;PodList&quot;,&quot;apiVersion&quot;:&quot;v1&quot;,&quot;metadata&quot;:{},&quot;items&quot;:[{&quot;metadata&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;web-1&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The kubelet&#39;s configuration allowed anonymous requests and used \u003ccode>AlwaysAllow\u003c/code> authorization. Setting anonymous to false, authorization mode to Webhook and restarting the kubelet made the same request return 401. The benchmark check turned from FAIL to PASS, which is the verification the task wanted.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Editing the API server manifest without a backup copy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting to restart the kubelet after changing its config\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Granting cluster-admin for convenience\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving the default service account token mounted in pods that do not need it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fixing a benchmark check and not re-running it\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"default-deny-and-metadata","text":"Default deny and metadata"},{"id":"cis-benchmark-with-kube-bench","text":"CIS benchmark with kube-bench"},{"id":"harden-the-api-server","text":"Harden the API server"},{"id":"harden-the-kubelet","text":"Harden the kubelet"},{"id":"rbac-and-service-accounts","text":"RBAC and service accounts"},{"id":"protect-ingress-and-secrets-in-transit","text":"Protect ingress and secrets in transit"},{"id":"verify-the-binaries","text":"Verify the binaries"},{"id":"keep-up-with-versions","text":"Keep up with versions"},{"id":"incident-anonymous-access-left-on","text":"Incident: Anonymous access left on"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: securing a cluster","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/securing-a-cluster/"},{"t":"CIS Kubernetes Benchmark","u":"https://www.cisecurity.org/benchmark/kubernetes"},{"t":"Kubernetes: kubelet authentication and authorization","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/kubelet-authn-authz/"}],"text":"The Certified Kubernetes Security Specialist exam assumes you can already administer a cluster, which is why it requires a current CKA. Cluster setup and cluster hardening together make about a quarter of it. The idea is to take a working cluster and remove the ways it can be abused. The DevSecOps module covers the principles; here are the tasks. What you will learn Default deny network policy and protecting node metadata Running and reading CIS benchmark results Hardening API server and kubelet settings RBAC, service accounts and verifying binaries Default deny and metadata Start with a default deny policy in each namespace, then allow only what is needed. A deny-all for ingress and egress was shown in the networking lesson. Cloud nodes often expose a metadata service at a link-local address, which holds credentials for the node&#39;s cloud role. Pods should usually not reach it: apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: deny-metadata namespace: shop spec: podSelector: {} policyTypes: - Egress egress: - to: - ipBlock: cidr: 0.0 .0 .0 /0 except: - 169.254 .169 .254 /32 This allows all egress except to the metadata address. Combine it with other policies as the task requires, remembering that policies add up. CIS benchmark with kube-bench The CIS Kubernetes Benchmark is a long list of recommended settings, and kube-bench is a tool that checks a node against it. kube-bench run --targets master kube-bench run --targets node kube-bench run --targets master --check 1.2.1 The output lists each check as PASS, FAIL or WARN, with remediation text. A task usually names a failing check and asks you to fix it. The fix is almost always a flag in a static pod manifest or a kubelet configuration file. Re-run the check afterwards to confirm. Harden the API server Edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml ; the kubelet restarts the pod when you save. Useful settings: --anonymous-auth=false rejects unauthenticated requests --authorization-mode=Node,RBAC enables node and role-based authorization, not AlwaysAllow --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction limits what kubelets can change --profiling=false disables profiling endpoints --audit-log-path and related flags enable auditing (a later lesson) Avoid insecure ports and unneeded features After a change, wait for the API server to return. If it does not, read crictl logs for the container; the cause is usually a typo. Keep a copy of the original manifest outside the manifests directory before editing, so that you can restore it quickly. Harden the kubelet The kubelet configuration is a file, often /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml . Important settings: authentication: anonymous: enabled: false webhook: enabled: true authorization: mode: Webhook readOnlyPort: 0 Anonymous access disabled, authorization delegated to the API server and the read-only port closed. Apply with systemctl restart kubelet . Etcd and the control plane should also use client certificate authentication, which kubeadm sets up by default. RBAC and service accounts Least privilege applies to humans and to workloads. Avoid cluster-admin bindings and wildcard verbs or resources Look for risky bindings with kubectl get clusterrolebinding -o wide Remove permissions that are not needed, and bind roles to groups instead of many individuals Set automountServiceAccountToken: false on pods or service accounts that do not call the API Use dedicated service accounts per workload, not the default account kubectl patch serviceaccount default -n shop -p &#x27;{\"automountServiceAccountToken\": false}&#x27; kubectl auth can-i --list --as system:serviceaccount:shop:default -n shop Protect ingress and secrets in transit Expose applications over TLS. Create a secret from a certificate and key, and reference it in the Ingress tls section: kubectl create secret tls web-tls --cert=tls.crt --key=tls.key -n shop Verify the binaries A tampered binary defeats every other control. Compare the checksum of a downloaded Kubern"},"k8s-exam/cks-system-hardening-apparmor-seccomp-and-the-host":{"html":"\u003cp>Containers share the host kernel. If an attacker escapes a container, the host&#39;s configuration decides how much they can reach. System hardening is the set of controls on the host and on the kernel interface that limit that damage. It is about fifteen percent of the CKS.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reducing the attack surface of the node\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Loading and applying AppArmor profiles\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Applying seccomp profiles to pods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Other kernel and network controls\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"shrink-the-host\">Shrink the host\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A node should run what it needs and nothing more. Habits and checks:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ss -ltnp\nsystemctl list-units --type=service --state=running\ndpkg -l | wc -l\nsystemctl disable --now snapd\napt-get remove --purge -y PACKAGE\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ss -ltnp\nsystemctl list-units --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>=service --state=running\ndpkg -l | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\nsystemctl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">disable\u003c/span> --now snapd\napt-get remove --purge -y PACKAGE\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Look for listening ports you do not recognise, services that are not part of the node&#39;s job, and tools that help an attacker such as compilers or network scanners. Remove or disable them. Restrict SSH: key authentication only, no root login, and limit who can connect. Remove users you do not need, and keep the node patched. A task may give you a listing and ask which unused service to stop.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"apparmor\">AppArmor\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>AppArmor is a Linux security module that confines a program by a profile: which files it may read or write, which capabilities and network access it has. A profile must exist on the node where the pod runs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aa-status\ncat /etc/apparmor.d/k8s-deny-write\napparmor_parser -q /etc/apparmor.d/k8s-deny-write\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">aa-status\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /etc/apparmor.d/k8s-deny-write\napparmor_parser -q /etc/apparmor.d/k8s-deny-write\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A simple profile that denies writes everywhere:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#include &lt;tunables/global&gt;\nprofile k8s-deny-write flags=(attach_disconnected) {\n  #include &lt;abstractions/base&gt;\n  file,\n  deny /** w,\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">#include &lt;tunables/global&gt;\nprofile k8s-deny-write flags=(attach_disconnected) {\n  #include &lt;abstractions/base&gt;\n  file,\n  deny /** w,\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Load it on every node that may run the pod, with \u003ccode>apparmor_parser\u003c/code>. Then reference it. In Kubernetes 1.30 and later, the pod field is:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"spec:\n  containers:\n    - name: app\n      image: nginx:1.27\n      securityContext:\n        appArmorProfile:\n          type: Localhost\n          localhostProfile: k8s-deny-write\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">app\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx:1.27\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">securityContext:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">appArmorProfile:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Localhost\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">localhostProfile:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">k8s-deny-write\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Older clusters and many tutorials use an annotation instead, \u003ccode>container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/app: localhost/k8s-deny-write\u003c/code>. Check which form the cluster version supports. If the profile is not loaded on the node, the pod fails to start, which is a useful signal. Verify with \u003ccode>kubectl exec\u003c/code> that the restriction holds, for example that writing a file fails.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"seccomp\">Seccomp\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Seccomp filters which system calls a process may make. The default runtime profile blocks many dangerous calls while allowing normal behaviour.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"spec:\n  securityContext:\n    seccompProfile:\n      type: RuntimeDefault\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">securityContext:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">seccompProfile:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">RuntimeDefault\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>RuntimeDefault\u003c/code> applies the container runtime&#39;s default profile, which is a good baseline for almost all workloads. \u003ccode>Unconfined\u003c/code> disables filtering and should be avoided. A custom profile is a JSON file placed under the kubelet&#39;s seccomp directory, commonly \u003ccode>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp\u003c/code>, and referenced by a path relative to it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"securityContext:\n  seccompProfile:\n    type: Localhost\n    localhostProfile: profiles/audit.json\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">securityContext:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">seccompProfile:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Localhost\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">localhostProfile:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">profiles/audit.json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The profile file must exist on the node that runs the pod. A task might give you a profile and ask you to apply it to a pod, then show the pod running. If the pod stays in an error state, the events usually say the profile could not be found.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capabilities-and-privileges\">Capabilities and privileges\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Containers should drop what they do not need:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"securityContext:\n  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\n  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true\n  runAsNonRoot: true\n  capabilities:\n    drop: [&quot;ALL&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">securityContext:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">allowPrivilegeEscalation:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">readOnlyRootFilesystem:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runAsNonRoot:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">capabilities:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">drop:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ALL&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Avoid \u003ccode>privileged: true\u003c/code>, host namespaces and host paths. Each one removes a layer of isolation. The pod security lesson shows how to enforce this across a namespace.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"kernel-and-network-controls\">Kernel and network controls\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Disable kernel modules that are not needed by blacklisting them, and keep sysctl settings tight. Limit which nodes can talk to each other and to the control plane with firewall rules or cloud security groups, and give nodes the least cloud permissions needed. A node role that can read all storage buckets turns a container escape into a data breach.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-host-hardening-checklist\">A host hardening checklist\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"checklist = {\n    &quot;no unneeded listening ports&quot;: True,\n    &quot;ssh root login disabled&quot;: True,\n    &quot;unused services stopped&quot;: False,\n    &quot;apparmor profile loaded for sensitive pods&quot;: True,\n    &quot;seccomp RuntimeDefault on pods&quot;: False,\n}\nprint(&quot;to do:&quot;, [k for k, ok in checklist.items() if not ok])\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">checklist = {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;no unneeded listening ports&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ssh root login disabled&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;unused services stopped&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;apparmor profile loaded for sensitive pods&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;seccomp RuntimeDefault on pods&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>,\n}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;to do:&quot;\u003c/span>, [k \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> k, ok \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> checklist.items() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> ok])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"host&gt;kern;kern&gt;aa;kern&gt;sec;aa&gt;ctr;sec&gt;ctr;ctr&gt;chk\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Layers of confinement\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"host\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Hardened host\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"kern\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:16.7%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Kernel controls\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"aa\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">AppArmor profile\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sec\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Seccomp profile\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ctr\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Container\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"chk\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Hardened host to Kernel controls; 2, Kernel controls to AppArmor profile; 3, Kernel controls to Seccomp profile; 4, AppArmor profile to Container; 5, Seccomp profile to Container; 6, Container to Verify.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"host\">\u003cdt>Hardened host \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Few services, patched, limited SSH.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unused services left running.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"kern\">\u003cdt>Kernel controls \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Modules and sysctl limits.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Default permissive settings.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"aa\">\u003cdt>AppArmor profile \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> File and capability limits.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Profile not loaded on the node.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sec\">\u003cdt>Seccomp profile \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> System call filtering.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unconfined profile.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ctr\">\u003cdt>Container \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Non-root, no privilege escalation.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Privileged mode.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"chk\">\u003cdt>Verify \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Exec and test the restriction.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Assuming the profile applies.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-profile-that-was-not-there\">Incident: The profile that was not there\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A pod referencing an AppArmor profile would not start on one node, and the same manifest worked on another.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pod locked -o wide\nkubectl describe pod locked | grep -i apparmor\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pod locked -o wide\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME     READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE   NODE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">locked   0/1     Blocked   0          1m    w2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pod locked | grep -i apparmor\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  Warning  Failed  kubelet  Cannot enforce AppArmor: profile &quot;k8s-deny-write&quot; is not loaded\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The profile had been loaded on \u003ccode>w1\u003c/code> but not on \u003ccode>w2\u003c/code>. Loading it on every node that could schedule the pod, with a configuration management step to keep them in sync, fixed the problem. If a task has only one node, you only need to load it there; in a larger cluster, remember that the pod may land elsewhere.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Referencing an AppArmor profile that is not loaded on the node\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Loading a profile on one node and scheduling elsewhere\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using Unconfined seccomp when RuntimeDefault would do\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not testing that the restriction actually works\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving unused services and open ports on nodes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"shrink-the-host","text":"Shrink the host"},{"id":"apparmor","text":"AppArmor"},{"id":"seccomp","text":"Seccomp"},{"id":"capabilities-and-privileges","text":"Capabilities and privileges"},{"id":"kernel-and-network-controls","text":"Kernel and network controls"},{"id":"a-host-hardening-checklist","text":"A host hardening checklist"},{"id":"incident-the-profile-that-was-not-there","text":"Incident: The profile that was not there"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: AppArmor","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/security/apparmor/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: seccomp","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/security/seccomp/"}],"text":"Containers share the host kernel. If an attacker escapes a container, the host&#39;s configuration decides how much they can reach. System hardening is the set of controls on the host and on the kernel interface that limit that damage. It is about fifteen percent of the CKS. What you will learn Reducing the attack surface of the node Loading and applying AppArmor profiles Applying seccomp profiles to pods Other kernel and network controls Shrink the host A node should run what it needs and nothing more. Habits and checks: ss -ltnp systemctl list-units -- type =service --state=running dpkg -l | wc -l systemctl disable --now snapd apt-get remove --purge -y PACKAGE Look for listening ports you do not recognise, services that are not part of the node&#39;s job, and tools that help an attacker such as compilers or network scanners. Remove or disable them. Restrict SSH: key authentication only, no root login, and limit who can connect. Remove users you do not need, and keep the node patched. A task may give you a listing and ask which unused service to stop. AppArmor AppArmor is a Linux security module that confines a program by a profile: which files it may read or write, which capabilities and network access it has. A profile must exist on the node where the pod runs. aa-status cat /etc/apparmor.d/k8s-deny-write apparmor_parser -q /etc/apparmor.d/k8s-deny-write A simple profile that denies writes everywhere: #include \u003ctunables/global> profile k8s-deny-write flags=(attach_disconnected) { #include \u003cabstractions/base> file, deny /** w, } Load it on every node that may run the pod, with apparmor_parser . Then reference it. In Kubernetes 1.30 and later, the pod field is: spec: containers: - name: app image: nginx:1.27 securityContext: appArmorProfile: type: Localhost localhostProfile: k8s-deny-write Older clusters and many tutorials use an annotation instead, container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/app: localhost/k8s-deny-write . Check which form the cluster version supports. If the profile is not loaded on the node, the pod fails to start, which is a useful signal. Verify with kubectl exec that the restriction holds, for example that writing a file fails. Seccomp Seccomp filters which system calls a process may make. The default runtime profile blocks many dangerous calls while allowing normal behaviour. spec: securityContext: seccompProfile: type: RuntimeDefault RuntimeDefault applies the container runtime&#39;s default profile, which is a good baseline for almost all workloads. Unconfined disables filtering and should be avoided. A custom profile is a JSON file placed under the kubelet&#39;s seccomp directory, commonly /var/lib/kubelet/seccomp , and referenced by a path relative to it: securityContext: seccompProfile: type: Localhost localhostProfile: profiles/audit.json The profile file must exist on the node that runs the pod. A task might give you a profile and ask you to apply it to a pod, then show the pod running. If the pod stays in an error state, the events usually say the profile could not be found. Capabilities and privileges Containers should drop what they do not need: securityContext: allowPrivilegeEscalation: false readOnlyRootFilesystem: true runAsNonRoot: true capabilities: drop: [ \"ALL\" ] Avoid privileged: true , host namespaces and host paths. Each one removes a layer of isolation. The pod security lesson shows how to enforce this across a namespace. Kernel and network controls Disable kernel modules that are not needed by blacklisting them, and keep sysctl settings tight. Limit which nodes can talk to each other and to the control plane with firewall rules or cloud security groups, and give nodes the least cloud permissions needed. A node role that can read all storage buckets turns a container escape into a data breach. A host hardening checklist checklist = { \"no unneeded listening ports\" : True , \"ssh root login disabled\" : True , \"unused services stopped\" : False , \"apparmor profile loaded for sensitiv"},"k8s-exam/cks-minimize-microservice-vulnerabilities":{"html":"\u003cp>This is the largest CKS domain, about a fifth of the exam. The topic is the security of the workloads themselves and the data they handle. The common thread is defence in depth: assume one layer will fail and make sure the next one holds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Enforcing Pod Security Standards with admission labels\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Security context settings that matter\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sandboxed runtimes with RuntimeClass\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Encryption of Secrets at rest, and mutual TLS between services\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pod-security-standards-and-admission\">Pod Security Standards and admission\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Kubernetes defines three profiles. \u003cstrong>Privileged\u003c/strong> is unrestricted. \u003cstrong>Baseline\u003c/strong> blocks known privilege escalations, such as host namespaces and privileged containers. \u003cstrong>Restricted\u003c/strong> follows current hardening practice, requiring non-root users, dropped capabilities and a seccomp profile. The built-in Pod Security admission controller enforces them per namespace through labels:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl label namespace shop pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted\nkubectl label namespace shop pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted\nkubectl get namespace shop --show-labels\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl label namespace shop pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted\nkubectl label namespace shop pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted\nkubectl get namespace shop --show-labels\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The modes are \u003ccode>enforce\u003c/code> (reject), \u003ccode>warn\u003c/code> (allow with a warning) and \u003ccode>audit\u003c/code> (log). Start with warn to see what would break. A task may ask you to label a namespace and then fix a deployment so its pods are admitted. The rejection message lists which fields violate the profile.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"security-context\">Security context\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A restricted-compliant pod looks like this:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: hardened\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  securityContext:\n    runAsNonRoot: true\n    runAsUser: 10001\n    seccompProfile:\n      type: RuntimeDefault\n  containers:\n    - name: app\n      image: nginx:1.27\n      securityContext:\n        allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\n        readOnlyRootFilesystem: true\n        capabilities:\n          drop: [&quot;ALL&quot;]\n      volumeMounts:\n        - name: tmp\n          mountPath: /tmp\n  volumes:\n    - name: tmp\n      emptyDir: {}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Pod\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">hardened\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">securityContext:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runAsNonRoot:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runAsUser:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10001\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">seccompProfile:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">RuntimeDefault\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">app\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx:1.27\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">securityContext:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">allowPrivilegeEscalation:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">readOnlyRootFilesystem:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">capabilities:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">drop:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ALL&quot;\u003c/span>]\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumeMounts:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">tmp\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mountPath:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/tmp\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumes:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">tmp\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">emptyDir:\u003c/span> {}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A read-only root filesystem breaks programs that write to disk, so give them an \u003ccode>emptyDir\u003c/code> for the paths they need. Many stock images need changes to run as non-root, for example listening on a port above 1024.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sandboxed-runtimes\">Sandboxed runtimes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Ordinary containers share the host kernel. For untrusted or multi-tenant code, a sandbox adds a second boundary, such as gVisor, which implements a user-space kernel, or Kata Containers, which uses a lightweight VM. In Kubernetes, you define a RuntimeClass and reference it from the pod.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: node.k8s.io/v1\nkind: RuntimeClass\nmetadata:\n  name: gvisor\nhandler: runsc\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">node.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">RuntimeClass\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">gvisor\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">handler:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">runsc\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"spec:\n  runtimeClassName: gvisor\n  containers:\n    - name: untrusted\n      image: nginx:1.27\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runtimeClassName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">gvisor\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">untrusted\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx:1.27\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The handler name must match one configured in the container runtime on the node. If it does not, the pod fails with an event about the missing handler. Sandboxing has a performance cost and does not support every feature, so use it where the risk justifies it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secrets-handling-and-encryption-at-rest\">Secrets: handling and encryption at rest\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Secrets are base64 encoded by default and stored unencrypted in etcd unless you enable encryption. Anyone with etcd access, or a backup, can read them. Enable encryption with an \u003ccode>EncryptionConfiguration\u003c/code> file and an API server flag.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1\nkind: EncryptionConfiguration\nresources:\n  - resources:\n      - secrets\n    providers:\n      - aescbc:\n          keys:\n            - name: key1\n              secret: &lt;base64-encoded 32 byte key&gt;\n      - identity: {}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">EncryptionConfiguration\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">secrets\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">providers:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">aescbc:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">keys:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">key1\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">secret:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&lt;base64-encoded\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">32\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">byte\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">key&gt;\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">identity:\u003c/span> {}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Pass \u003ccode>--encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml\u003c/code> to the API server, and mount the file into the static pod. The first provider is used for writing; \u003ccode>identity\u003c/code> last lets the server still read old unencrypted data. Existing secrets are only encrypted when rewritten, so replace them:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Verify by reading a secret directly from etcd; the stored value should start with \u003ccode>k8s:enc:aescbc:v1:\u003c/code> instead of plain text. Production setups usually use a key management service provider so the key never sits on disk.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Also reduce exposure in use: mount secrets as files rather than environment variables when possible, limit who can read them with RBAC, and do not put them in images or configuration maps.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"traffic-between-services\">Traffic between services\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Inside the cluster, traffic is plain unless you protect it. Mutual TLS gives each service an identity and encrypts the connection. A service mesh or a CNI with encryption features can provide it. For the exam, know the purpose and be able to read a policy that requires it, and know that network policies control who may connect while mTLS controls who is talking and hides the contents.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"admission-and-policy-engines\">Admission and policy engines\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Policy engines such as Open Policy Agent Gatekeeper and Kyverno enforce custom rules on objects at admission: required labels, allowed registries, banned settings. Tasks may give you a constraint template and ask you to create a constraint that applies it to a namespace, then show a violating pod being denied.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pod = {&quot;runAsNonRoot&quot;: True, &quot;allowPrivilegeEscalation&quot;: False, &quot;readOnlyRootFilesystem&quot;: True,\n       &quot;capabilities_drop&quot;: [&quot;ALL&quot;], &quot;seccomp&quot;: &quot;RuntimeDefault&quot;, &quot;privileged&quot;: False}\nproblems = []\nif not pod[&quot;runAsNonRoot&quot;]: problems.append(&quot;runs as root&quot;)\nif pod[&quot;allowPrivilegeEscalation&quot;]: problems.append(&quot;privilege escalation allowed&quot;)\nif pod[&quot;capabilities_drop&quot;] != [&quot;ALL&quot;]: problems.append(&quot;capabilities not dropped&quot;)\nif pod[&quot;privileged&quot;]: problems.append(&quot;privileged container&quot;)\nprint(problems or &quot;meets the restricted profile&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">pod = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;runAsNonRoot&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;allowPrivilegeEscalation&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;readOnlyRootFilesystem&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>,\n       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;capabilities_drop&quot;\u003c/span>: [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ALL&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;seccomp&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;RuntimeDefault&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;privileged&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>}\nproblems = []\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> pod[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;runAsNonRoot&quot;\u003c/span>]: problems.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;runs as root&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> pod[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;allowPrivilegeEscalation&quot;\u003c/span>]: problems.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;privilege escalation allowed&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> pod[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;capabilities_drop&quot;\u003c/span>] != [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ALL&quot;\u003c/span>]: problems.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;capabilities not dropped&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> pod[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;privileged&quot;\u003c/span>]: problems.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;privileged container&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(problems \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">or\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;meets the restricted profile&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"adm&gt;ctx;ctx&gt;run;ctx&gt;sec;run&gt;net;sec&gt;net;net&gt;wl\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Layers around one workload\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"adm\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Admission\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ctx\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Security context\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"run\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Runtime class\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sec\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Encrypted secrets\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"net\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Network and mTLS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"wl\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Workload\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Admission to Security context; 2, Security context to Runtime class; 3, Security context to Encrypted secrets; 4, Runtime class to Network and mTLS; 5, Encrypted secrets to Network and mTLS; 6, Network and mTLS to Workload.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"adm\">\u003cdt>Admission \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pod Security labels and policy engine.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Namespace without labels.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ctx\">\u003cdt>Security context \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Non-root, no escalation, dropped capabilities.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Defaults left permissive.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"run\">\u003cdt>Runtime class \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sandbox for untrusted code.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Handler not installed on the node.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sec\">\u003cdt>Encrypted secrets \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Encryption at rest and tight RBAC.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Keys stored with the data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"net\">\u003cdt>Network and mTLS \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Allowed paths and encrypted connections.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Plain traffic between pods.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"wl\">\u003cdt>Workload \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The hardened service.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Trusting one layer only.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-secrets-in-the-clear\">Incident: Secrets in the clear\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An engineer assumed secrets were safe in the cluster. A backup of etcd was copied to a shared disk, and a quick search revealed a database password in plain text.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get /registry/secrets/shop/db --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key | strings | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get /registry/secrets/shop/db --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key | strings | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/registry/secrets/shop/db\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">password\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">s3cret\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The secret was stored unencrypted. The team enabled encryption at rest, rewrote all secrets, rotated the exposed password and restricted who could read etcd backups. Reading the value back with etcdctl and seeing the \u003ccode>k8s:enc:\u003c/code> prefix confirmed the fix.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Enabling encryption but not rewriting existing secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enforcing a Pod Security profile without checking what would break first\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting a read-only root filesystem without writable paths for temp files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Referencing a RuntimeClass whose handler is not installed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating base64 as protection\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"pod-security-standards-and-admission","text":"Pod Security Standards and admission"},{"id":"security-context","text":"Security context"},{"id":"sandboxed-runtimes","text":"Sandboxed runtimes"},{"id":"secrets-handling-and-encryption-at-rest","text":"Secrets: handling and encryption at rest"},{"id":"traffic-between-services","text":"Traffic between services"},{"id":"admission-and-policy-engines","text":"Admission and policy engines"},{"id":"incident-secrets-in-the-clear","text":"Incident: Secrets in the clear"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: pod security admission","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-admission/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: encrypting secret data at rest","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/encrypt-data/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: runtime class","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/runtime-class/"}],"text":"This is the largest CKS domain, about a fifth of the exam. The topic is the security of the workloads themselves and the data they handle. The common thread is defence in depth: assume one layer will fail and make sure the next one holds. What you will learn Enforcing Pod Security Standards with admission labels Security context settings that matter Sandboxed runtimes with RuntimeClass Encryption of Secrets at rest, and mutual TLS between services Pod Security Standards and admission Kubernetes defines three profiles. Privileged is unrestricted. Baseline blocks known privilege escalations, such as host namespaces and privileged containers. Restricted follows current hardening practice, requiring non-root users, dropped capabilities and a seccomp profile. The built-in Pod Security admission controller enforces them per namespace through labels: kubectl label namespace shop pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted kubectl label namespace shop pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted kubectl get namespace shop --show-labels The modes are enforce (reject), warn (allow with a warning) and audit (log). Start with warn to see what would break. A task may ask you to label a namespace and then fix a deployment so its pods are admitted. The rejection message lists which fields violate the profile. Security context A restricted-compliant pod looks like this: apiVersion: v1 kind: Pod metadata: name: hardened namespace: shop spec: securityContext: runAsNonRoot: true runAsUser: 10001 seccompProfile: type: RuntimeDefault containers: - name: app image: nginx:1.27 securityContext: allowPrivilegeEscalation: false readOnlyRootFilesystem: true capabilities: drop: [ \"ALL\" ] volumeMounts: - name: tmp mountPath: /tmp volumes: - name: tmp emptyDir: {} A read-only root filesystem breaks programs that write to disk, so give them an emptyDir for the paths they need. Many stock images need changes to run as non-root, for example listening on a port above 1024. Sandboxed runtimes Ordinary containers share the host kernel. For untrusted or multi-tenant code, a sandbox adds a second boundary, such as gVisor, which implements a user-space kernel, or Kata Containers, which uses a lightweight VM. In Kubernetes, you define a RuntimeClass and reference it from the pod. apiVersion: node.k8s.io/v1 kind: RuntimeClass metadata: name: gvisor handler: runsc spec: runtimeClassName: gvisor containers: - name: untrusted image: nginx:1.27 The handler name must match one configured in the container runtime on the node. If it does not, the pod fails with an event about the missing handler. Sandboxing has a performance cost and does not support every feature, so use it where the risk justifies it. Secrets: handling and encryption at rest Secrets are base64 encoded by default and stored unencrypted in etcd unless you enable encryption. Anyone with etcd access, or a backup, can read them. Enable encryption with an EncryptionConfiguration file and an API server flag. apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1 kind: EncryptionConfiguration resources: - resources: - secrets providers: - aescbc: keys: - name: key1 secret: \u003cbase64-encoded 32 byte key> - identity: {} Pass --encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml to the API server, and mount the file into the static pod. The first provider is used for writing; identity last lets the server still read old unencrypted data. Existing secrets are only encrypted when rewritten, so replace them: kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f - Verify by reading a secret directly from etcd; the stored value should start with k8s:enc:aescbc:v1: instead of plain text. Production setups usually use a key management service provider so the key never sits on disk. Also reduce exposure in use: mount secrets as files rather than environment variables when possible, limit who can read them with RBAC, and do not put them in images or configuration maps. Traffic between services Ins"},"k8s-exam/cks-supply-chain-security":{"html":"\u003cp>Supply chain security is another fifth of the CKS. The idea is that the cluster is only as safe as the images, charts and manifests it runs. The DevSecOps module covers the principles of provenance. This lesson is about the tools and tasks you will meet.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Smaller, safer images and Dockerfile habits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scanning images with Trivy and acting on results\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restricting registries and tags through admission\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Static analysis of manifests, and signatures\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"minimise-the-image\">Minimise the image\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every package in an image is something to patch and something an attacker can use. Good habits:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Use a small base image, such as a minimal or distroless one\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use multi-stage builds so compilers and build tools do not ship\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run as a non-root user\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pin base images by version, ideally by digest\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Do not copy secrets into layers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Remove shells and package managers from runtime images where practical\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>A Dockerfile reviewed in a task might use \u003ccode>latest\u003c/code>, run as root and install extra tools. Fix those lines.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"dockerfile\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"FROM golang:1.23 AS build\nWORKDIR /src\nCOPY . .\nRUN CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -o /app ./cmd/app\n\nFROM gcr.io/distroless/static:nonroot\nCOPY --from=build /app /app\nUSER nonroot\nENTRYPOINT [&quot;/app&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> golang:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1.23\u003c/span> AS build\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WORKDIR\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> /src\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> . .\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">RUN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -o /app ./cmd/app\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> gcr.io/distroless/static:nonroot\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> --from=build /app /app\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">USER\u003c/span> nonroot\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ENTRYPOINT\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/app&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scan-images\">Scan images\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Trivy is a common scanner for images, file systems and configuration. It lists known vulnerabilities by severity.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"trivy image nginx:1.21\ntrivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL nginx:1.21\ntrivy image --ignore-unfixed --severity CRITICAL nginx:1.27\ntrivy image -f json -o result.json nginx:1.27\ntrivy config ./deploy\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">trivy image nginx:1.21\ntrivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL nginx:1.21\ntrivy image --ignore-unfixed --severity CRITICAL nginx:1.27\ntrivy image -f json -o result.json nginx:1.27\ntrivy config ./deploy\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A typical task: scan several images used by pods in a namespace and delete the pods or deployments that use an image with a critical vulnerability, or choose the least vulnerable image. To list images in use:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -n shop -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{&quot;\\t&quot;}{.spec.containers[*].image}{&quot;\\n&quot;}{end}'\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get pods -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{&quot;\\t&quot;}{.spec.containers[*].image}{&quot;\\n&quot;}{end}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Remember that a scan is a snapshot. New vulnerabilities are published every day, so scan again on a schedule and in the pipeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pin-by-digest\">Pin by digest\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A tag such as \u003ccode>1.27\u003c/code> can point to different content over time. A digest cannot.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"containers:\n  - name: app\n    image: nginx@sha256:0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">app\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx@sha256:0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Get the digest with the registry or with \u003ccode>crictl images --digests\u003c/code>. Pinning gives reproducible deployments and prevents a swapped image under the same tag.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"allow-only-trusted-registries\">Allow only trusted registries\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You can block images from unknown places at admission. Options in the curriculum include:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>ImagePolicyWebhook\u003c/strong>, an admission plugin that asks an external service whether an image is allowed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>OPA Gatekeeper\u003c/strong> with a constraint that allows only certain repository prefixes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Kyverno\u003c/strong> policies that match image names and require digests or signatures\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>With Gatekeeper, you apply a ConstraintTemplate, then a Constraint naming the namespaces and allowed prefixes. After creating them, test by trying to run an image from another registry and confirm that the API rejects it with the policy message.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get constrainttemplates\nkubectl get k8sallowedrepos\nkubectl run bad --image=docker.io/library/busybox:1.36 -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get constrainttemplates\nkubectl get k8sallowedrepos\nkubectl run bad --image=docker.io/library/busybox:1.36 -n shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If you configure ImagePolicyWebhook, you edit the API server manifest to enable the plugin and provide an admission configuration file with a kubeconfig for the webhook. Keep a backup of the manifest, because a mistake here can stop pods from being admitted at all.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"static-analysis\">Static analysis\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Check manifests for risky settings before deploying. Tools such as kubesec score a manifest, and Trivy can scan configuration for misconfigurations, such as privileged containers, missing limits or host paths.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubesec scan pod.yaml\ntrivy config ./manifests\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubesec scan pod.yaml\ntrivy config ./manifests\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A task may ask you to fix the findings, then re-scan to show improvement. Common fixes: set a security context, drop capabilities, add resource limits and avoid host namespaces.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"signatures-and-provenance\">Signatures and provenance\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Signing an image proves who built it and that it has not changed. The Sigstore project provides cosign for signing and verifying, and the SLSA framework describes levels of build integrity. For the exam, know what a signature and an SBOM, the software bill of materials, are for, and how an admission policy can require verified signatures. Also know that your CI system and registry credentials are part of the chain and need the same protection as the cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"allowed = (&quot;registry.example.com/&quot;, &quot;gcr.io/distroless/&quot;)\nimages = [&quot;registry.example.com/shop/web:1.4&quot;, &quot;docker.io/library/nginx:latest&quot;, &quot;gcr.io/distroless/static:nonroot&quot;]\nfor img in images:\n    ok = img.startswith(allowed) and not img.endswith(&quot;:latest&quot;)\n    print((&quot;ALLOW &quot; if ok else &quot;DENY  &quot;) + img)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">allowed = (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;registry.example.com/&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;gcr.io/distroless/&quot;\u003c/span>)\nimages = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;registry.example.com/shop/web:1.4&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;docker.io/library/nginx:latest&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;gcr.io/distroless/static:nonroot&quot;\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> img \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> images:\n    ok = img.startswith(allowed) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">and\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> img.endswith(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;:latest&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>((\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ALLOW &quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> ok \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DENY  &quot;\u003c/span>) + img)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"src&gt;bld;bld&gt;scan;scan&gt;reg;reg&gt;adm;adm&gt;run\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">From source to running image\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"src\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Source and Dockerfile\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"bld\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Build\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"scan\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Scan and sign\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"reg\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:16.7%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Registry\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"adm\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:83.3%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Admission\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"run\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cluster\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Source and Dockerfile to Build; 2, Build to Scan and sign; 3, Scan and sign to Registry; 4, Registry to Admission; 5, Admission to Cluster.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"src\">\u003cdt>Source and Dockerfile \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Minimal, non-root, pinned base.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Latest tags and extra tools.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"bld\">\u003cdt>Build \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Multi-stage build, no secrets in layers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Secrets baked into layers.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"scan\">\u003cdt>Scan and sign \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Vulnerability scan and a signature.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Scan results ignored.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"reg\">\u003cdt>Registry \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Trusted store with access control.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Public or shared credentials.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"adm\">\u003cdt>Admission \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Allowed registries, digests, signatures.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Policy not enforced for all namespaces.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"run\">\u003cdt>Cluster \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Only approved images start.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unscanned images deployed by hand.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-image-from-a-lookalike-registry\">Incident: The image from a lookalike registry\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A pod started from an image with a name almost identical to an internal one, pulled from a public registry, and ran an unexpected process.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pod report -n shop -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].image}'\nkubectl get pod report -n shop -o jsonpath='{.metadata.labels}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pod report -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.containers[0].image}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">docker.io/shop/report:2.3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pod report -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.metadata.labels}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;app&quot;:&quot;report&quot;}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The manifest had omitted the internal registry prefix, so the runtime pulled a similarly named public image. After this the team required fully qualified image names, added a policy that allowed only the internal registry prefix, and required digests for production namespaces. Testing a deliberately bad image proved that the policy denied it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating one scan as permanent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Allowing the latest tag or short image names\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enabling an admission plugin without a backup of the API server manifest\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fixing the Dockerfile but not rebuilding and redeploying the image\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring the build system and registry as part of the attack surface\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"minimise-the-image","text":"Minimise the image"},{"id":"scan-images","text":"Scan images"},{"id":"pin-by-digest","text":"Pin by digest"},{"id":"allow-only-trusted-registries","text":"Allow only trusted registries"},{"id":"static-analysis","text":"Static analysis"},{"id":"signatures-and-provenance","text":"Signatures and provenance"},{"id":"incident-the-image-from-a-lookalike-registry","text":"Incident: The image from a lookalike registry"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Trivy documentation","u":"https://trivy.dev/latest/docs/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: admission controllers","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/admission-controllers/"}],"text":"Supply chain security is another fifth of the CKS. The idea is that the cluster is only as safe as the images, charts and manifests it runs. The DevSecOps module covers the principles of provenance. This lesson is about the tools and tasks you will meet. What you will learn Smaller, safer images and Dockerfile habits Scanning images with Trivy and acting on results Restricting registries and tags through admission Static analysis of manifests, and signatures Minimise the image Every package in an image is something to patch and something an attacker can use. Good habits: Use a small base image, such as a minimal or distroless one Use multi-stage builds so compilers and build tools do not ship Run as a non-root user Pin base images by version, ideally by digest Do not copy secrets into layers Remove shells and package managers from runtime images where practical A Dockerfile reviewed in a task might use latest , run as root and install extra tools. Fix those lines. FROM golang: 1.23 AS build WORKDIR /src COPY . . RUN CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -o /app ./cmd/app FROM gcr.io/distroless/static:nonroot COPY --from=build /app /app USER nonroot ENTRYPOINT [ \"/app\" ] Scan images Trivy is a common scanner for images, file systems and configuration. It lists known vulnerabilities by severity. trivy image nginx:1.21 trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL nginx:1.21 trivy image --ignore-unfixed --severity CRITICAL nginx:1.27 trivy image -f json -o result.json nginx:1.27 trivy config ./deploy A typical task: scan several images used by pods in a namespace and delete the pods or deployments that use an image with a critical vulnerability, or choose the least vulnerable image. To list images in use: kubectl get pods -n shop -o jsonpath= &#x27;{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{\"\\t\"}{.spec.containers[*].image}{\"\\n\"}{end}&#x27; Remember that a scan is a snapshot. New vulnerabilities are published every day, so scan again on a schedule and in the pipeline. Pin by digest A tag such as 1.27 can point to different content over time. A digest cannot. containers: - name: app image: nginx@sha256:0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef Get the digest with the registry or with crictl images --digests . Pinning gives reproducible deployments and prevents a swapped image under the same tag. Allow only trusted registries You can block images from unknown places at admission. Options in the curriculum include: ImagePolicyWebhook , an admission plugin that asks an external service whether an image is allowed OPA Gatekeeper with a constraint that allows only certain repository prefixes Kyverno policies that match image names and require digests or signatures With Gatekeeper, you apply a ConstraintTemplate, then a Constraint naming the namespaces and allowed prefixes. After creating them, test by trying to run an image from another registry and confirm that the API rejects it with the policy message. kubectl get constrainttemplates kubectl get k8sallowedrepos kubectl run bad --image=docker.io/library/busybox:1.36 -n shop If you configure ImagePolicyWebhook, you edit the API server manifest to enable the plugin and provide an admission configuration file with a kubeconfig for the webhook. Keep a backup of the manifest, because a mistake here can stop pods from being admitted at all. Static analysis Check manifests for risky settings before deploying. Tools such as kubesec score a manifest, and Trivy can scan configuration for misconfigurations, such as privileged containers, missing limits or host paths. kubesec scan pod.yaml trivy config ./manifests A task may ask you to fix the findings, then re-scan to show improvement. Common fixes: set a security context, drop capabilities, add resource limits and avoid host namespaces. Signatures and provenance Signing an image proves who built it and that it has not changed. The Sigstore project provides cosign for signing and verifying, and the SLSA framework describes levels of build integrity. For t"},"k8s-exam/cks-monitoring-runtime-security-and-the-mock-run":{"html":"\u003cp>The last CKS domain is about seeing attacks while they happen and after. It is also about 20 percent of the exam. This lesson covers audit logs, runtime detection with Falco, immutability and investigation, and ends with a method for running a full practice exam under time pressure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Kubernetes audit logging and how to enable it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Falco rules and reading alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Immutable containers and spotting suspicious pods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A plan for a timed mock exam\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"audit-logging\">Audit logging\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The API server can record who did what and when. You choose how much to log with an audit policy, which lists rules matched in order. Levels are \u003ccode>None\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Metadata\u003c/code> (who and what, not the body), \u003ccode>Request\u003c/code> (adds the request body) and \u003ccode>RequestResponse\u003c/code> (adds the response). A small policy:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: audit.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Policy\nomitStages:\n  - RequestReceived\nrules:\n  - level: RequestResponse\n    resources:\n      - group: &quot;&quot;\n        resources: [&quot;secrets&quot;]\n  - level: Metadata\n    resources:\n      - group: &quot;&quot;\n        resources: [&quot;pods&quot;, &quot;configmaps&quot;]\n  - level: None\n    users: [&quot;system:kube-proxy&quot;]\n  - level: Metadata\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">audit.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Policy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">omitStages:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">RequestReceived\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">level:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">RequestResponse\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">group:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;secrets&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">level:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Metadata\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">group:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pods&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;configmaps&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">level:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">None\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">users:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;system:kube-proxy&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">level:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Metadata\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Rules are evaluated top to bottom and the first match wins, so order matters. Logging secret bodies is sensitive, so most real policies use \u003ccode>Metadata\u003c/code> for secrets. To enable the policy on a kubeadm cluster, edit the API server manifest:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /root/kube-apiserver.yaml.bak\nvim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cp\u003c/span> /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /root/kube-apiserver.yaml.bak\nvim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Add flags and the volumes that make the files reachable inside the pod:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"    - --audit-policy-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit/policy.yaml\n    - --audit-log-path=/var/log/kubernetes/audit/audit.log\n    - --audit-log-maxage=7\n    - --audit-log-maxbackup=3\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--audit-policy-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit/policy.yaml\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--audit-log-path=/var/log/kubernetes/audit/audit.log\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--audit-log-maxage=7\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--audit-log-maxbackup=3\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Then add \u003ccode>volumeMounts\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>volumes\u003c/code> entries for \u003ccode>/etc/kubernetes/audit\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>/var/log/kubernetes/audit\u003c/code>. Without them the API server cannot read the policy and will not start. After it restarts, search the log:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep '&quot;resource&quot;:&quot;secrets&quot;' /var/log/kubernetes/audit/audit.log | tail -n 3\njq 'select(.verb==&quot;delete&quot;) | .user.username' /var/log/kubernetes/audit/audit.log | sort | uniq -c\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">grep \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;&quot;resource&quot;:&quot;secrets&quot;&#x27;\u003c/span> /var/log/kubernetes/audit/audit.log | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -n 3\njq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;select(.verb==&quot;delete&quot;) | .user.username&#x27;\u003c/span> /var/log/kubernetes/audit/audit.log | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uniq\u003c/span> -c\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"falco\">Falco\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Falco watches system calls on a node and raises alerts when behaviour matches a rule, such as a shell starting inside a container or a sensitive file being read. It runs as a service on each node or as a daemon set. Default rules live in \u003ccode>/etc/falco/falco_rules.yaml\u003c/code>, and your own go in \u003ccode>/etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml\u003c/code>, which survives upgrades.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A rule has a name, a description, a condition, an output and a priority:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"- rule: Shell in container\n  desc: A shell was started inside a container\n  condition: evt.type = execve and container.id != host and proc.name in (bash, sh)\n  output: &quot;Shell started in container (user=%user.name container=%container.name image=%container.image.repository command=%proc.cmdline)&quot;\n  priority: WARNING\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rule:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Shell\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">in\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">container\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">desc:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">A\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shell\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">was\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">started\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">inside\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">a\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">container\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">condition:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">evt.type\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">execve\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">and\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">container.id\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">!=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">host\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">and\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">proc.name\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">in\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">(bash,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">sh)\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">output:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Shell started in container (user=%user.name container=%container.name image=%container.image.repository command=%proc.cmdline)&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">priority:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">WARNING\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Read alerts with \u003ccode>journalctl -u falco -f\u003c/code> or from the configured output file. A task might say: find the pod that spawned a shell in the last few minutes, or change a rule&#39;s output format, then confirm that an alert shows the new format after triggering it with \u003ccode>kubectl exec\u003c/code>. After editing rules, restart the service and check for syntax errors in its log.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"immutable-containers\">Immutable containers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Attackers install tools and change files after getting in. If the root filesystem is read-only and containers do not run privileged, much of that fails. Review a namespace for containers that break the rule:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -n shop -o json | jq -r '.items[] | select(.spec.containers[].securityContext.privileged==true) | .metadata.name'\nkubectl get pods -n shop -o json | jq -r '.items[] | select(.spec.containers[].securityContext.readOnlyRootFilesystem != true) | .metadata.name'\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get pods -n shop -o json | jq -r \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.items[] | select(.spec.containers[].securityContext.privileged==true) | .metadata.name&#x27;\u003c/span>\nkubectl get pods -n shop -o json | jq -r \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.items[] | select(.spec.containers[].securityContext.readOnlyRootFilesystem != true) | .metadata.name&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Fix by setting \u003ccode>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true\u003c/code> and adding writable \u003ccode>emptyDir\u003c/code> mounts for specific paths. Do not use \u003ccode>hostPath\u003c/code>, host networking or host PID unless the task justifies it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"investigate-a-suspicious-pod\">Investigate a suspicious pod\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A method that works: identify the pod and node, check what it runs and as whom, review recent audit events, check Falco alerts, inspect the image, and then contain it by isolating with a network policy, scaling down the owner and preserving evidence before deleting. Do not delete first; you lose the evidence.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pod odd -n shop -o yaml | grep -E &quot;image:|serviceAccount|hostPath|privileged&quot;\nkubectl logs odd -n shop\nkubectl exec odd -n shop -- ps aux\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get pod odd -n shop -o yaml | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;image:|serviceAccount|hostPath|privileged&quot;\u003c/span>\nkubectl logs odd -n shop\nkubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> odd -n shop -- ps aux\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"plan-a-full-mock-run\">Plan a full mock run\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Practise under real conditions, because the second half of the exam is where unpractised candidates slip.\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Set up.\u003c/strong> Start a timer for the real duration. Use only the permitted docs.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Triage first.\u003c/strong> Spend three minutes listing the tasks with weights and rough effort.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Quick points first.\u003c/strong> Do short, high-weight tasks, then medium ones.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Flag at the time cap.\u003c/strong> If a task takes more than about twice its expected time, flag it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Verify each result.\u003c/strong> Run the check command before moving on.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Reserve the last ten minutes.\u003c/strong> Return to flagged tasks and re-read their wording.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Review afterwards.\u003c/strong> For each missed task, write what you would do next time, and drill it.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Run at least two full mocks per exam, a week apart, and compare timings per domain.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"mock = [\n    (&quot;etcd backup&quot;, 7, 8, 7),\n    (&quot;fix kubelet&quot;, 8, 12, 15),\n    (&quot;network policy&quot;, 5, 7, 6),\n    (&quot;pod security&quot;, 6, 9, 9),\n]\nfor name, weight, planned, actual in mock:\n    flag = &quot;over&quot; if actual &gt; planned * 1.5 else &quot;ok&quot;\n    print(f&quot;{name:16} weight {weight}  planned {planned}  actual {actual}  {flag}&quot;)\nprint(&quot;score weight done:&quot;, sum(w for _, w, _, _ in mock))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">mock = [\n    (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;etcd backup&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">7\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">7\u003c/span>),\n    (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;fix kubelet&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">12\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">15\u003c/span>),\n    (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;network policy&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">7\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">6\u003c/span>),\n    (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pod security&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">6\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">9\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">9\u003c/span>),\n]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> name, weight, planned, actual \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> mock:\n    flag = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;over&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> actual &gt; planned * \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1.5\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ok&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{name:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">16\u003c/span>}\u003c/span> weight \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{weight}\u003c/span>  planned \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{planned}\u003c/span>  actual \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{actual}\u003c/span>  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{flag}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;score weight done:&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> _, w, _, _ \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> mock))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"aud&gt;inv;fal&gt;inv;inv&gt;con;con&gt;imm;imm&gt;mock\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Seeing and containing an attack\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"aud\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:16.7%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Audit log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fal\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:83.3%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Falco alerts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"inv\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Investigate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"con\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Contain\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"imm\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Immutable fix\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mock\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Mock run review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Audit log to Investigate; 2, Falco alerts to Investigate; 3, Investigate to Contain; 4, Contain to Immutable fix; 5, Immutable fix to Mock run review.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"aud\">\u003cdt>Audit log \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Who did what through the API.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Policy logging nothing useful.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fal\">\u003cdt>Falco alerts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Suspicious behaviour on nodes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Rules never tested.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"inv\">\u003cdt>Investigate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pod, node, process, image and account.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Deleting before preserving evidence.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"con\">\u003cdt>Contain \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Isolate with policy, scale down owner.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slow or partial isolation.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"imm\">\u003cdt>Immutable fix \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Read-only root and no privilege.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The same weakness left in place.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mock\">\u003cdt>Mock run review \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Timings, misses and drills.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never practising under time.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-audit-policy-that-broke-the-api-server\">Incident: The audit policy that broke the API server\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A candidate enabled audit logging, and the API server never came back.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"crictl ps -a | grep kube-apiserver\ncrictl logs a91c3e 2&gt;&amp;1 | tail -n 2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>crictl ps -a | grep kube-apiserver\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">a91c3e  kube-apiserver  Exited  3 minutes ago\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>crictl logs a91c3e 2&gt;&amp;1 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -n 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error: failed to load audit policy file: open /etc/kubernetes/audit/policy.yaml: no such file or directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The flags were added but the audit directory was not mounted into the API server pod, so the process could not read the policy file. Adding the \u003ccode>volumes\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>volumeMounts\u003c/code> entries, and restoring the backup first to get kubectl working again, fixed it. This is why you copy the manifest before editing and check the container log if the pod does not return.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Adding audit flags without the volume mounts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logging secret bodies at RequestResponse level in real clusters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing the default Falco rules file instead of the local one\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting a suspicious pod before looking at it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Doing only single tasks in practice and never a full timed run\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"audit-logging","text":"Audit logging"},{"id":"falco","text":"Falco"},{"id":"immutable-containers","text":"Immutable containers"},{"id":"investigate-a-suspicious-pod","text":"Investigate a suspicious pod"},{"id":"plan-a-full-mock-run","text":"Plan a full mock run"},{"id":"incident-the-audit-policy-that-broke-the-api-server","text":"Incident: The audit policy that broke the API server"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: auditing","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-cluster/audit/"},{"t":"Falco documentation","u":"https://falco.org/docs/"}],"text":"The last CKS domain is about seeing attacks while they happen and after. It is also about 20 percent of the exam. This lesson covers audit logs, runtime detection with Falco, immutability and investigation, and ends with a method for running a full practice exam under time pressure. What you will learn Kubernetes audit logging and how to enable it Falco rules and reading alerts Immutable containers and spotting suspicious pods A plan for a timed mock exam Audit logging The API server can record who did what and when. You choose how much to log with an audit policy, which lists rules matched in order. Levels are None , Metadata (who and what, not the body), Request (adds the request body) and RequestResponse (adds the response). A small policy: apiVersion: audit.k8s.io/v1 kind: Policy omitStages: - RequestReceived rules: - level: RequestResponse resources: - group: \"\" resources: [ \"secrets\" ] - level: Metadata resources: - group: \"\" resources: [ \"pods\" , \"configmaps\" ] - level: None users: [ \"system:kube-proxy\" ] - level: Metadata Rules are evaluated top to bottom and the first match wins, so order matters. Logging secret bodies is sensitive, so most real policies use Metadata for secrets. To enable the policy on a kubeadm cluster, edit the API server manifest: cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /root/kube-apiserver.yaml.bak vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml Add flags and the volumes that make the files reachable inside the pod: - --audit-policy-file=/etc/kubernetes/audit/policy.yaml - --audit-log-path=/var/log/kubernetes/audit/audit.log - --audit-log-maxage=7 - --audit-log-maxbackup=3 Then add volumeMounts and volumes entries for /etc/kubernetes/audit and /var/log/kubernetes/audit . Without them the API server cannot read the policy and will not start. After it restarts, search the log: grep &#x27;\"resource\":\"secrets\"&#x27; /var/log/kubernetes/audit/audit.log | tail -n 3 jq &#x27;select(.verb==\"delete\") | .user.username&#x27; /var/log/kubernetes/audit/audit.log | sort | uniq -c Falco Falco watches system calls on a node and raises alerts when behaviour matches a rule, such as a shell starting inside a container or a sensitive file being read. It runs as a service on each node or as a daemon set. Default rules live in /etc/falco/falco_rules.yaml , and your own go in /etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml , which survives upgrades. A rule has a name, a description, a condition, an output and a priority: - rule: Shell in container desc: A shell was started inside a container condition: evt.type = execve and container.id != host and proc.name in (bash, sh) output: \"Shell started in container (user=%user.name container=%container.name image=%container.image.repository command=%proc.cmdline)\" priority: WARNING Read alerts with journalctl -u falco -f or from the configured output file. A task might say: find the pod that spawned a shell in the last few minutes, or change a rule&#39;s output format, then confirm that an alert shows the new format after triggering it with kubectl exec . After editing rules, restart the service and check for syntax errors in its log. Immutable containers Attackers install tools and change files after getting in. If the root filesystem is read-only and containers do not run privileged, much of that fails. Review a namespace for containers that break the rule: kubectl get pods -n shop -o json | jq -r &#x27;.items[] | select(.spec.containers[].securityContext.privileged==true) | .metadata.name&#x27; kubectl get pods -n shop -o json | jq -r &#x27;.items[] | select(.spec.containers[].securityContext.readOnlyRootFilesystem != true) | .metadata.name&#x27; Fix by setting readOnlyRootFilesystem: true and adding writable emptyDir mounts for specific paths. Do not use hostPath , host networking or host PID unless the task justifies it. Investigate a suspicious pod A method that works: identify the pod and node, check what it runs and as whom, review recent audit events, check Falco alerts,"}});